ID: Ch10-Intro
TEXT:

 

"Twice in two days," Hadrik mutters from the dark. "First woodland critters, now overgrown brutes. If this gets back to the arena circuit, I'll never hear the end of it."

Six hours. The small phosphor screen on my stat device gives the exact count, glowing like a pale green ember in the pitch black. Without it, we would have no sense of time at all. There are no windows in this cell, no sconces, no cracks in the mortared stone. Just cold, damp air and silence.

For six hours, we have been testing the paralysis ward woven into the flagstones. It sits on our nerves like lead weights. Daren and Hadrik tried forcing their way through it earlier, channeling raw magic to jolt their muscles into a brief spasm, but three clumsy strides were all they managed before the floor's enchantment locked their joints solid again.

My lessathi blood at least spares my fingers. Along with a stubborn tolerance for electric shocks, I inherited enough resistance to ward off complete immobility. It is not much of an advantage—I cannot stand, let alone run—but it lets me reach into my pocket and angle the device screen upward like a weak lantern.

Talking the guards into opening the heavy oak door went nowhere. Dim-witted or not, the ogres fear their captain far more than our bribes. They have strict orders to leave us alone in the dark.

Rose sits curled against the far wall, motionless, without uttering a word since we were thrown in. Illuna's sudden betrayal hit her hardest. The rest of us are stiff, bruised, and miserable, but we are not waiting around to die. The ogres know our collars carry lethal timers; sooner or later, someone has to bring food.

The plan is simple enough: the instant the latch lifts, Daren and Hadrik trigger another muscle jolt, bowl over the guards, and sprint for the corridor's ward switch before the paralysis resets. It is crude and desperate, but sitting here in the dark is worse.

"Treacherous little parasite," Daren growls, his teeth clenched against the numbness. "I knew the moment she showed her face in the ruins. We needed gear, we needed a guide, so I ignored my better judgment. I should have driven my blade through that banshee the second she woke."

choice("Yeah, she sure did a number on us.", Ch10-Number, v_current_scene = Ch10-Number, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)
choice("Actually, I'm not so sure that she really betrayed us.", Ch10-Betrayed, v_current_scene = Ch10-Betrayed, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch10-Number
TEXT:

 "Yeah, she sure did a number on us."

"I am not convinced she did," Kate's voice cuts in from the shadow beside me.

"You think this is part of her goodwill tour?" I ask.

"Listen to what she told the shaman about our collars," Kate says, her tone crisp and analytical. "Lessathi engineering is advanced, but compact explosive runes have strict physical limits. A casing that small could clear a single room, maybe two. It cannot collapse a mountain fortress. Illuna knows how little ogres understand ancient tech. She fed them an outrageous lie to keep them from slitting our throats on the spot."

"A comforting thought, lass," Hadrik says, "but it sounds like wishful thinking. You honestly expect the banshee to waltz down here, throw open the door, and tell us it was all part of the act?"

Heavy boots scrape outside our cell.

"Halt!" an ogre barks in the hall. "No pass! Turn around or—"

The voice cuts off with a wet snap. Something heavy drops against the wood outside.

"Look at that!" Arraka's voice bleeds through the oak planks, shrill with three overlaid cadences of cruel delight. "He was thinking so hard about that sentence, he didn't even notice the blade taking his head off!"

"Quiet," Illuna snaps.

"Oh, unlock this locket and you'll never hear another peep from me!" Arraka cackles.

The leaden weight in my shoulders lifts. Sensation rushes back into my legs with a prickle of pins and needles. The heavy door groans on rusted iron hinges, swinging inward. 

Illuna stands in the threshold. Flower's small frame, Flower's red hair, but the face is set in stone and the eyes glow ice-blue.

"On your feet," Illuna says flatly. "The chalice will not steal itself."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Sword, v_current_scene = Ch10-Sword)


ID: Ch10-Betrayed
TEXT:

 "Actually, I'm not so sure that she really betrayed us."

"Have you lost your mind?" Daren snaps.

"Think about what she told them," I say, rolling my wrists to shake the stiffness. "She warned the shaman about the five-day deadline, but she never mentioned the proximity tether. If she wanted us dead, she could have told them to drag us twenty paces apart and let the shock finish the job. She bought time so she could wander the compound unhindered."

"You are grasping at straws, Barry," Daren mutters. "She probably figured an explosion was cleaner. Starving us out keeps the fort intact."

"Barry is right about the blast," Kate says from the dark. "Lessathi engineering is advanced, but compact explosive runes have strict physical limits. A casing that small could clear a single room, maybe two. It cannot collapse a mountain fortress. Illuna knows how little ogres understand ancient tech. She fed them an outrageous lie to keep them from slitting our throats on the spot."

"A comforting thought, lass," Hadrik says, "but it sounds like wishful thinking. You honestly expect the banshee to waltz down here, throw open the door, and tell us it was all part of the act?"

Heavy boots scrape outside our cell.

"Halt!" an ogre barks in the hall. "No pass! Turn around or—"

The voice cuts off with a wet snap. Something heavy drops against the wood outside.

"Look at that!" Arraka's voice bleeds through the oak planks, shrill with three overlaid cadences of cruel delight. "He was thinking so hard about that sentence, he didn't even notice the blade taking his head off!"

"Quiet," Illuna snaps.

"Oh, unlock this locket and you'll never hear another peep from me!" Arraka cackles.

The leaden weight in my shoulders lifts. Sensation rushes back into my legs with a prickle of pins and needles. The heavy door groans on rusted iron hinges, swinging inward. 

Illuna stands in the threshold. Flower's small frame, Flower's red hair, but the face is set in stone and the eyes glow ice-blue.

"On your feet," Illuna says flatly. "The chalice will not steal itself."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Sword, v_current_scene = Ch10-Sword)


ID: Ch10-Sword
TEXT:

 None of us move right away. Illuna narrows her eyes.

"The girl told you we had a plan before we walked in here," she says, her voice clipped. "This is the plan. I am breaking you out. Stand up. Standing in open corridors gets people killed."

"I am not buying it," Daren says, pushing himself off the stone. "Your bargain with the beasts fell through, and now you need us to fight your way out. If this was your plan from the start, you would have briefed us."

"I could not brief you, you idiot," Illuna retorts. "The shaman reads auras. If you were acting, your spiritual pulses would have given the game away in half a second. That is why I put Flower to sleep. I am the only one here who suppresses emotions and aura shifts completely."

"Is that so?" Daren steps toward her, his jaw rigid. "Then wake the girl up. Let her tell us."

"The stasis spell locks her mind for two days," Illuna says. "Breaking it early causes permanent damage. I had no intention of waiting around for forty-eight hours while you rot."

"How convenient."

Illuna's jaw tightens. "If you prefer the cell, by all means, stay. I will take the others and leave you to the guards."

"Oh, I am coming," Daren says, his voice dropping to a low, dangerous register. "And the moment I have my steel back, you and I are settling this."

"Fascinating," Illuna says. She flicks two fingers. A gentle ripple of water currents lifts our confiscated gear from the hall floor and floats it through the doorway.

My crossbow and dagger drop neatly into my lap. Kate catches her hunting knife by the hilt without blinking. Illuna's fingers close around Daren's broadsword. Daren lunges forward, but his legs are still clumsy from the lingering paralysis; he stumbles, catching himself against the doorframe.

Illuna tracks him with cold blue eyes, then flips the hilt and tosses the broadsword to me.

"Take it," she says. "You possess a functioning brain, and you know him better than I do. Decide for yourself whether he is in any state to hold a weapon."

"Barry..." Daren turns toward me, extending an open hand. "Give me my sword."

His eyes have that blind, righteous fury in them. He is not listening to reason; he is ready to gut her in the hallway before we even take three steps.

choice("Give him the sword", Ch10-Give, v_current_scene = Ch10-Give)
choice("Only give him the sword if he swears to not attack Illuna with it", Ch10-Swear, v_current_scene = Ch10-Swear)


ID: Ch10-Give
TEXT:

 "Do what you want," I say, tossing the hilt toward his grip.

"Predictable," Illuna mutters.

"Our turn, banshee," Daren says, rolling his shoulders as his fingers lock around the leather wrap. "No trickery this time."

He strides out into the torchlit corridor, past the headless ogre slumped against the masonry. The rest of us follow, edging against the damp walls. Nobody moves to intervene.

"We finish this," Daren says, squaring his stance on the stone flags. "To the end. Ready?"

"Always," Illuna says, deadpan.

Daren closes the distance with a roaring charge, raising his blade for a heavy cleave.

A whip of condensed water erupts from Illuna's left palm. It cracks across Daren's wrist with the sound of breaking ice. The impact forces his fingers open, sending the broadsword clattering across the stones. Before he can recover, her right hand snaps a second water coil straight around his armored waist. The liquid hardens into rigid ice. With a sharp flick of her wrist, she uses his own momentum to pivot and slam him flat against the stone wall.

Daren hits the mortar hard, the breath knocked out of him. When he scrambles to his knees, a spearhead of crystalline water is hovering an inch from his throat. The edge glints like honed steel.

"How..." Daren wheezes, staring up at her. "That physical force... it is impossible."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Give2, v_current_scene = Ch10-Give2)


ID: Ch10-Give2
TEXT:

 "Of course it is," Illuna says. "The whip carried the kinetic torque; my arm only guided the trajectory. You assumed a child's frame could not generate leverage, lowered your center of gravity, and handed me the angle. Arrogance makes men predictable."

She lets the water spear hold him against the stone for another beat.

"Now then," she continues. "I have no patience for heroics, but for Flower's sake, I am willing to let you live. Swear you will keep your steel sheathed until we clear the stronghold, and you may have your weapon back."

Daren stares at the razor-thin tip hovering at his collarbone. "You are sparing me?"

"You are a creature of vows," Illuna says. "If you give your word, you will die before breaking it. Give it, and we move."

Daren grits his teeth. "I swear it. Not a finger laid on you until we are outside these walls."

Illuna drops her hand. The water spear dissolves into vapor.

"The great white mage of the east, humbled by a slip of a girl," Hadrik says with a low rumble of a chuckle, leaning against the wall as Daren retrieves his blade. "Aren't banshees supposed to be your specialty, lad?"

"My foot slipped," Daren mutters, wiping grit from his breastplate. "I would have countered if I had my shield."

"Indeed," Illuna says. She raises her right hand.

A loose brick in the corridor wall pops free with a dry grind. Daren's heavy kite shield floats smoothly out of the cavity and hovers into his waiting grip.

Daren stares at it. "You had it stashed in the wall?"

"I hid it there before opening your cell," she says calmly. "I knew your temper would outrun your intellect. Now, if the duel is finished, we have a treasury to rob. Move."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Apologizing, v_current_scene = Ch10-Apologizing)


ID: Ch10-Swear
TEXT:

 "On one condition," I say, keeping the crossguard out of his reach. "Give your word you will not use it on Illuna."

"Again with this?" Daren says. "Don't tell me that you actually believe her lies?"

"It doesn't matter what I think," I say. "Either way you look at it, this isn't the time to be fighting each other. Now, are you going to swear it or not?"

Daren frowns at me.

"Fine, I'll swear it!" he says. "But only until we're all at a safe distance from the ogre fort."

"Good enough for me," I say, and I toss him the sword.

As the five of us exit our cell, Illuna makes a gesture with her right hand, and a stone dislodges itself from a nearby wall. Daren's shield then comes floating out of the hole in the wall, and into Daren's hand.

"I don't understand," Daren says. "Why did the ogres place my shield inside of a wall?"

"They didn't," Illuna says. "It was stashed in the same place as all the other weapons. I hid it here before unlocking your cell to make sure I had the upper hand, in case you decided to challenge me again, like you did last night. Now, then, if we're all done here, I suggest we get going. I already found out where they're holding the chalice."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Apologizing, v_current_scene = Ch10-Apologizing)


ID: Ch10-Apologizing
TEXT:

 "Is that all?" Rose steps forward, her voice shaking with rare, raw fury, tears brimming at the corners of her eyes. "You lock us in the dark for six hours, and that is all you have to say?"

"I told you," Illuna says, her tone unyielding. "Your shock had to be genuine. The shaman reads spiritual resonance. Any deception would have ruined the infiltration."

"And the insults?" Rose's voice cracks. "Telling me I was a gullible fool for trusting you? That I should have listened to Daren? Was that part of the grand plan as well?"

Illuna's cold demeanor falters. For a fraction of a second, the mask slips, revealing something exposed and deeply weary.

"No," Illuna says quietly. "It was not. I was irritated from being kept awake during the night watch. I lost my temper. I apologize."

Rose blinks, caught off balance by the direct admission. The anger in her face drains away, replaced by bewilderment. 

"Just... do not do it again," Rose murmurs.

Arraka's screech of laughter cuts through the corridor like jagged glass.

"An apology!" the banshee howls from the locket. "Take cover! Build barricades! The heavens are falling!"

"Quiet, Arraka," Illuna mutters.

"Go on, snap the lid shut then!" Arraka taunts. "Do it! See what happens to that precious little projection we spent three hours mapping in the archives! You need my eyes, little girl! Ahahaha!"

Between Illuna's palms, a glowing, semi-transparent construct hovers in the gloom—a scaled, rotating blueprint of the entire stone stronghold. Tiny points of light drift along its hallways: red markers pacing regular routes, blue markers clustered near the lower levels, and six stationary white dots in our corridor.

"You built this from the library records?" Kate asks, stepping closer to study the floating projection.

"Arraka did," Illuna says. "Her sensory range encompasses the entire fortress. Even with the shaman's cloaking wards masking their auras, she tracks their physical heat and bloodflow, translating them directly onto the grid. That is how I cleared this wing without raising an alarm."

choice(""Why do the dots have different colors?"", Ch10-Colors, v_current_scene = Ch10-Colors)
choice(""Can't Arraka maintain the hologram if you close the amulet?"", Ch10-Hologram, v_current_scene = Ch10-Hologram)


ID: Ch10-Colors
TEXT:

 "Why do the dots have different colors?"

"Blue are mages," Illuna says. "Red are line warriors, green is the shaman, and the purple marker near the central shaft is the captain."

"Arraka distinguishes ranks by aura alone?" Kate asks.

"Spiritual mass and elemental affinities leave distinct fingerprints," Illuna says. "The layout is clear. The treasury sits on the lowest subterranean tier. We move now."

"Hold on," Hadrik rumbles, crossing his thick arms. "Before we charge into the depths, clear something up for me. Back at the gate, the shaman called you 'Illuna of the sacred woods massacre.' Said you butchered hundreds of forest guardians. What was he talking about?"

"Oh, let me tell that one!" Arraka's voice bubbles up from the gold locket, dripping with vicious glee.

"Arraka, hold your tongue," Illuna warns.

"Or what?" Arraka mocks. "You'll pout?"

Illuna stays silent, her jaw set.

"That's what I thought!" Arraka sneers. "A few centuries back, out of the sheer kindness of my blackened heart, I took three newborn banshees under my wing. Sidara, Unuri, and our dear little Illuna here."

"You were their guardian?" Hadrik asks.

"Their mentor," Arraka purrs. "Their idol. They worshipped the ground I walked on! I taught them everything: how to evade white mages, how to reinforce their hosts, how to slip into a new body before the old one rejected their essence. I taught them how to survive."

"How generous of you," Kate says coldly.

"Wasn't it?" Arraka cackles. "Naturally, it was a setup! Wandering in animal bodies was growing dull, so I decided to raise three devoted daughters, earn their unconditional love, and crush them when the time was ripe. But Illuna... Illuna was my star pupil. Brilliant, disciplined, lethal. She reminded me of myself. So instead of slaughtering her with the others, I chose to mold her into my true successor."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Adorable, v_current_scene = Ch10-Adorable)


ID: Ch10-Hologram
TEXT:

 "Can't Arraka maintain the hologram if you close the amulet?"

"She claims breaking line-of-sight disrupts her focus," Illuna says, her tone flat. "A lie, but she will drop the construct the instant the clasp shuts. For now, the map is accurate. The treasury sits on the lowest subterranean tier. Follow this path."

"Hold on," Hadrik rumbles, crossing his thick arms. "Before we charge into the depths, clear something up for me. Back at the gate, the shaman called you 'Illuna of the sacred woods massacre.' Said you butchered hundreds of forest guardians. What was he talking about?"

"Oh, let me tell that one!" Arraka's voice bubbles up from the gold locket, dripping with vicious glee.

"Arraka, hold your tongue," Illuna warns.

"Or what?" Arraka mocks. "You'll pout?"

Illuna stays silent, her jaw set.

"That's what I thought!" Arraka sneers. "A few centuries back, out of the sheer kindness of my blackened heart, I took three newborn banshees under my wing. Sidara, Unuri, and our dear little Illuna here."

"You were their guardian?" Hadrik asks.

"Their mentor," Arraka purrs. "Their idol. They worshipped the ground I walked on! I taught them everything: how to evade white mages, how to reinforce their hosts, how to slip into a new body before the old one rejected their essence. I taught them how to survive."

"How generous of you," Kate says coldly.

"Wasn't it?" Arraka cackles. "Naturally, it was a setup! Wandering in animal bodies was growing dull, so I decided to raise three devoted daughters, earn their unconditional love, and crush them when the time was ripe. But Illuna... Illuna was my star pupil. Brilliant, disciplined, lethal. She reminded me of myself. So instead of slaughtering her with the others, I chose to mold her into my true successor."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Adorable, v_current_scene = Ch10-Adorable)


ID: Ch10-Adorable
TEXT:

 "Arraka, that is enough," Illuna says, her knuckles turning white.

"To forge a weapon, you must shatter the ore!" Arraka talks over her, her voice rising in pitch. "I drilled her in obscure water arts, perfected her form, and then marched all three into the sacred groves. They followed me blindly. They had no idea we were crossing into sacred territory. Unuri and Sidara were torn apart within minutes, and little Illuna was too weak to shield them both."

"Arraka, shut up!"

"She was so soft back then!" Arraka laughs, high and venomous. "Weeping for days whenever she had to take a host body! Even surrounded by raging forest guardians, she used nonlethal pulses, trying not to hurt the beasts! I watched from the canopy, laughing until my ribs ached. They screamed for me to save them. They couldn't fathom that their beloved mother was watching them die. And then the beasts ran Unuri and Sidara through."

Beside me, the air temperature plummets. Illuna's suppressed aura flares with violent, jagged pulses. It is the first time I have felt her lose control.

"She snapped," Arraka gloats. "Drowned the entire grove in a tempest of razored water. Sliced every guardian into ribbons, leaving the glade red. And then she crawled to the corpses, shaking them, begging them to wake up. 'Please! Don't leave me alone! I don't want to be alone—' Ahahaha!"

Snap.

Illuna slams the golden locket shut. Her fist trembles around the metal, the casing groaning under her grip. The floating blueprint flickers and dies, plunging the corridor back into torchlit gloom.

Illuna's chest rises and falls. She prises the locket open with a steady thumb.

"Restore it," she says, her voice deadly quiet.

"Restore what?" Arraka drawls from within. "The map? Oh, that took hours of channeling. You threw it away with your little fit. You'll just have to stumble through the dark and—"

"Restore it now." Illuna's voice hits like a lash of ice.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Treasury, v_current_scene = Ch10-Treasury)


ID: Ch10-Treasury
TEXT:

 A heavy silence hangs in the hall.

"Fine, fine," Arraka grumbles, and the glowing blue blueprint flares back to life between Illuna's hands, its moving dots intact. "No sense of drama. None at all."

Illuna turns her gaze on Hadrik. Her eyes are dark, hard slits.

"Does that satisfy your curiosity, dwarf? Or would you prefer a closer account?"

Hadrik shifts his weight, rubbing the back of his neck. "No. That's plenty."

Illuna spins on her heel and leads us down the spiral stone stairs.

With Arraka's projection lighting the way, we slip past patrol intersections like shadows. The few guards stationed at choke points never see what hits them. Illuna works with brutal economy: using Flower's small frame and acrobatic agility, she scales the damp masonry, hooks her knees over timber ceiling beams, and drops water blades from above with surgical precision. Clean incisions, zero noise. We drag each carcass into storage alcoves and keep descending.

Twenty minutes later, we reach the heavy reinforced iron doors of the lowest level. Two guards slump dead before they can reach for their horns.

Beyond the threshold lies the stronghold's vault.

Torches illuminate mounds of gold coins, silver ingots, and brass-bound chests overflowing with uncut gems. Without my enchanted backpack, I cannot haul out a fortune, but diamonds and cut rubies take up almost no room in a tunic pocket. In any port market on the Western Continent, a handful of those stones guarantees comfortable living for years. Larceny against ogres who threw us in a dungeon feels less like theft and more like standard hazard compensation.

choice("Take the gems", Ch10-Ambush, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ambush, v_ch10_gems = 1)
choice("Don't touch anything", Ch10-Ambush, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ambush, v_ch10_gems = 0)


ID: Ch10-Ambush
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_gems == 1) {
 I pry open a small brass coffer and slip a dozen cut diamonds and deep red rubies into my coat.

"Barry, really?" Daren mutters, scanning the shadows. "We are here for the chalice, not to loot like common thieves."

"There are tons of gold in this room," I whisper back, patting the pocket flat. "They will not miss a handful of rocks."

I hurry over to the center of the chamber, where the others stand around a black marble pedestal. Resting atop it is an ornate golden goblet, engraved with ancient script.

"Quiet," Illuna whispers, her eyes shining bright elven blue in Flower's small face. The glowing hologram of the stronghold hovers between her palms. "The purple dot is the captain. The red ones are his guard. They are sitting right behind that wall."

"That room isn't on the library plans," Kate murmurs.

"A hidden chamber," Illuna says. Her tone is flat, clinical. "No magical wards on the pedestal. Watch the corners."

"A goblet resting out in the open like that screams trap," Daren mutters, raising his shield.

"Is it even genuine?" Hadrik asks. "Could be a gilded decoy."

"A simple test," Illuna says. She raises an index finger; a thin stream of conjured water arcs through the air, filling the goblet to the brim.

The liquid glows with sudden, intense heat. A radiant golden aura flares from the metal, casting sharp white light across the stone vault before subsiding into a steady shimmer.

"It is the real artifact," Illuna says.

She lifts the chalice from the pedestal, dumping the water onto the flagstones. We freeze.

}
#if(v_ch10_gems == 0) {
 I leave the coffers alone and keep my hands clear. We step forward together, gathering around a black marble pedestal in the center of the vault. Resting atop it is an ornate golden goblet, engraved with ancient script.

"Quiet," Illuna whispers, her eyes shining bright elven blue in Flower's small face. The glowing hologram of the stronghold hovers between her palms. "The purple dot is the captain. The red ones are his guard. They are sitting right behind that wall."

"That room isn't on the library plans," Kate murmurs.

"A hidden chamber," Illuna says. Her tone is flat, clinical. "No magical wards on the pedestal. Watch the corners."

"A goblet resting out in the open like that screams trap," Daren mutters, raising his shield.

"Is it even genuine?" Hadrik asks. "Could be a gilded decoy."

"A simple test," Illuna says. She raises an index finger; a thin stream of conjured water arcs through the air, filling the goblet to the brim.

The liquid glows with sudden, intense heat. A radiant golden aura flares from the metal, casting sharp white light across the stone vault before subsiding into a steady shimmer.

"It is the real artifact," Illuna says.

She lifts the chalice from the pedestal, dumping the water onto the flagstones. We freeze.

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Ambush2, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ambush2)


ID: Ch10-Ambush2
TEXT:

 Silence presses in. No poisoned darts hiss from the ceiling. No runes flare along the floorboards.

Hadrik lets out a low snort and relaxes his shoulders. "Well. Give the brutes credit, I thought they were at least smart enough to—"

The floor drops an inch and spins.

Gears grind in the rock. The entire central flagstone ring pivots on a massive iron axle, swinging us through the back wall in a sickening blur of dust and screeching stone.

We halt in a torchlit bunker. A dozen massive ogres in iron mail surround us, weapons drawn. Standing at their center, towering over eight feet tall, the scarred ogre captain grins down at us with yellowed tusks.

"I suspected banshee from start," the captain rumbles in guttural, broken Common. "I told great shaman he make mistake for trusting banshee, but shaman no listen! I knew you come here to steal treasure. So I wait in secret ambush room. These are my finest warriors. No banshee or human leave this place alive tonight!"

"Is that a fact, fatso?" Arraka cackles from the locket.

Daren roars and lunges forward with his broadsword. The captain meets him mid-stride, swinging an iron-banded timber club with the speed of an uncoiling spring. Daren angles his kite shield to deflect, but the sheer kinetic shock hits like a battering ram. The impact cracks across the vault, launching Daren fifteen feet backward through the air until he collides hard with the masonry.

Hadrik charges next. The captain swings a downward blow meant to split a boulder. Hadrik catches the iron head between his palms, digging his heels into the mortar. The flagstones beneath their feet shatter into webbed fractures from the force.

Illuna moves like a shadow, flipping over a sweeping spear and snapping a water whip across the knees of two advancing guards.

The captain strains against Hadrik's lock, baring his teeth. "Not bad for puny dwarf! But tell me something. Dwarf know what lies beneath stronghold?"

"A hill," Hadrik grunts, his jaw clenched against the crushing weight. "Rocks and dirt."

"Abandoned goblin mines," the captain sneers. "Rotting timber scaffolding. Three hundred foot drop to the bottom. Can your friends fly, dwarf?"

The captain barks a guttural command. His guards disengage at once, scrambling toward the center ring. Illuna catches the movement instantly; her water whip snaps in a wide horizontal arc, the razor-thin stream shearing clean through four throats in a single sweep.

"Over here!" Illuna calls out, leaping clear of the falling bodies. "The foundation supports this section!"

The captain's grin widens. He wrenches his club free, kicks Hadrik back, and drives the heavy iron head straight down into the shattered stone beneath his boots.

choice("Rush over to Illuna's position. There is no time to waste.", Ch10-Rush, v_current_scene = Ch10-Rush)
choice("Tell Rose and Kate that we need to maintain our position.", Ch10-Maintain, v_current_scene = Ch10-Maintain)


ID: Ch10-Rush
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_perception >= 2 || v_premonition >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_perception < 2 && v_premonition < 2)
#if(v_premonition >= 2) {
 

We sprint toward Illuna as the captain's blow hammers the floor.

A sudden flare of foresight floods my vision: the stone beneath Illuna's feet disintegrating into dust, while the threshold ledge behind us holds firm.

"Back!" I yell, grabbing Kate's arm and shoving Rose behind me. "The center is rigged! Turn back!"

We pivot and dive for the solid rock near the door just as the floor gives way. Illuna catches the warning, vaults off the crumbling edge, kicks off a falling stone block, and lands lightly beside us on the surviving ledge.

The entire middle of the room drops into empty air with a deafening roar of falling masonry.

"Good eye," Illuna breathes, wiping dust from her brow.

"Daren!" Rose screams, pointing down into the dark chasm.

}
#if(v_perception >= 2 && v_premonition < 2) {
 

We sprint toward Illuna as the captain's blow hammers the floor.

As we run, I catch the fracture lines running through the mortar—the stress cracks radiate outward from the center, leaving the threshold perimeter undisturbed.

"Back!" I shout, grabbing Kate and pulling Rose toward the wall. "The center is the collapse zone! Get to the edge!"

We scramble back to the solid bedrock just as the floor shears away. Illuna reacts instantly, springing off a collapsing stone block and vaulting over the chasm to land beside us.

The entire middle section plummets into the dark abyss with a thunderous roar.

"Good eye," Illuna breathes, wiping dust from her brow.

"Daren!" Rose screams, pointing down into the dark chasm.

}
#if(v_perception < 2 && v_premonition < 2) {
 

We sprint straight for Illuna's position as the captain's club strikes home.

We reach the center just as the floor beneath our feet buckles. The fracture lines tear through the stones in every direction. There is no support underneath—only empty air.

"Turn back!" I yell, but my boots find no purchase.

The floor gives way entirely. The world drops out from beneath us, and the three-hundred-foot fall into the jagged rocks of the goblin mines snuffs out the light forever.

}
#if(v_perception >= 2 || v_premonition >= 2) {
 Thirty feet down the black shaft, Daren clings to a single rotted pine strut. The timber groans under the weight of his plate armor, splintering slowly from the cave wall. Below him, three hundred feet of sheer black drop leads to jagged mine debris. The old goblin scaffolding is nothing but rotted splinters and sheared uprights; there are no handholds to climb up.

"Stay back!" Daren shouts up through the swirling dust. "Do not come down here! I have everything under control!"

His fingers are slipping on the wet bark. The pine beam cracks louder. He has less than thirty seconds before it snaps.

Hadrik locks grips with the captain on the far ledge, roaring over the din. "You butcher! You lured your own men into the center!"

"Cannon fodder!" the captain spits, his club grinding against Hadrik's forearms. "I need no help to crush you. Watch your friend drop!"

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Teleport, v_current_scene = Ch10-Teleport) if (v_perception >= 2 || v_premonition >= 2)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_perception < 2 && v_premonition < 2)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_perception < 2 && v_premonition < 2)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_perception < 2 && v_premonition < 2)


ID: Ch10-Maintain
TEXT:

 "Hold your ground!" I shout, throwing an arm out to stop Rose and Kate. "It's a bait! Stay on the ledge!"

They freeze, trusting the call.

Illuna looks back in disbelief, but before she can speak, the captain's club crashes into the flagstones.

The floor shatters. The entire center ring shears away, tumbling into the abyss, while the solid bedrock ledge beneath our boots holds firm. Illuna rebounds off a falling block, kicks off the cave wall, and vaults across the chasm to land lightly beside us.

"Good eye," she says, steadying her breath.

"Daren!" Rose screams, rushing to the chasm's lip.

Thirty feet down the black shaft, Daren clings to a single rotted pine strut. The timber groans under the weight of his plate armor, splintering slowly from the cave wall. Below him, three hundred feet of sheer black drop leads to jagged mine debris. The old goblin scaffolding is nothing but rotted splinters and sheared uprights; there are no handholds to climb up.

"Stay back!" Daren shouts up through the swirling dust. "Do not come down here! I have everything under control!"

His fingers are slipping on the wet bark. The pine beam cracks louder. He has less than thirty seconds before it snaps.

Hadrik locks grips with the captain on the far ledge, roaring over the din. "You butcher! You lured your own men into the center!"

"Cannon fodder!" the captain spits, his club grinding against Hadrik's forearms. "I need no help to crush you. Watch your friend drop!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Teleport, v_current_scene = Ch10-Teleport)


ID: Ch10-Teleport
TEXT:

 The beam gives a loud, sharp crack.

Illuna dives over the ledge without hesitation. She ricochets off a vertical timber upright, kicks off the sheer granite wall, and drops thirty feet through the dark in three fluid rebounds. She catches a protruding ledge with one hand and reaches out toward Daren with the other.

"Take my hand!" she commands.

"Never!" Daren barks back.

The timber strut snaps with the sound of a bowshot.

"Take it, you idiot!"

Daren's grip tears free. He plummets. Illuna launches herself horizontally from the rock face, wrapping her arm around his armored collar as they both drop into the dark.

A pulse of brilliant white light flares deep in the shaft.

Before they hit the jagged rocks below, space folds with a sharp chime. Illuna and Daren materialize flat on the stone floor beside us, skidding to a halt on the safe ledge.

"Impossible!" the captain bellows across the gap, swinging his club in a fury.

"What's wrong?" Hadrik taunts, driving a shoulder into the ogre's ribs. "Thought you were handling us alone!"

"Can you strike him from range?" Rose asks Illuna urgently.

"Full anti-magic plate," Illuna says, breathing hard. "Water blades disperse on contact. He is too heavy to drag with a whip, and wide-area blasts will incinerate the dwarf. Hadrik has to break the armor physically."

"Hey, dwarf!" Arraka's voice shrills from the locket, cutting through the clang of steel. "Remember the ogre weak spot at the base of the neck? Use that secret technique I taught you!"

Hadrik grins, his teeth gleaming in the torchlight.

"Aye!" he roars. "Time to end this!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Quota, v_current_scene = Ch10-Quota)


ID: Ch10-Quota
TEXT:

 The captain instinctively pulls his club upward, tucking his chin and guarding his neck with both massive forearms.

Hadrik drops low. He funnels his concentrated aura into his right fist until the air hums, and drives a straight punch directly into the center of the ogre's iron breastplate.

The metal implodes with a deafening gong. Ribs shatter; the captain's eyes bulge as he collapses to his knees, coughing dark blood onto the stone. Hadrik kicks the dropped club over the precipice.

"Why..." the captain wheezes through bloody teeth, clutching his caved-in chest. "Neck... was weak point... why strike chest?"

Hadrik and Arraka both burst out laughing.

"There is no secret technique, you oversized melon!" Arraka howls. "I lied so you would leave your ribs wide open!"

"Always check your flanks, big guy," Hadrik says cheerfully.

The captain struggles to raise a bloodied fist. "Not... over... dwarf. I will tear your throat out and—"

Hadrik clamps both hands around the captain's skull and twists with a sickening crunch.

The massive body goes limp and rolls into the dark pit below. Hadrik leaps across the chasm, landing lightly beside us, dusting off his leather vest.

"Not bad, banshee," Hadrik tells the locket. "You earned your keep today."

"I could hardly let them kill my best audience," Arraka purrs. "You owe me an ale when I break out. Right before I slaughter the lot of you, of course! Ahahaha!"

"Are you insane?" Daren's voice shakes with sudden, furious outrage.

"Who, me?" Arraka asks.

"Not you!" Daren points a trembling finger at Illuna. "Her!"

Illuna dusts off her trousers, unimpressed. "Because I teleported you? Would you have preferred the three-hundred-foot drop?"

"My death would have solved all your problems!" Daren shouts, his face crimson. "No one would have questioned it! Why pull me out?"

"I owe you no explanations," Illuna says coldly. "Think what you like."

"Quiet, both of you," Kate cuts in, eyes fixed on Arraka's glowing map. "The barracks are emptying. Red markers are spreading through every corridor. The alarm is raised, and the shaman is moving with a strike team."

"Front gates are sealed," Illuna says, her focus snapping back instantly. "We cannot fight our way through the main halls. Our only route is downward, through the goblin mines to the hillside exit."

choice(""How do you know that the caverns below us won't lead to a dead end?"", Ch10-Caverns, v_current_scene = Ch10-Caverns)
choice(""How are we going to get all the way down there with the scaffolding broken?"", Ch10-Scaffolding, v_current_scene = Ch10-Scaffolding)


ID: Ch10-Caverns
TEXT:

 "How do you know that the caverns below us won't lead to a dead end?"

"We copied the mine schematics from the archives," Illuna says. "Arraka, expand the projection."

"Hold your horses," Arraka grunts.

The glowing blueprint shifts. A lower subterranean network unrolls beneath the fortress layout, displaying a long, winding tunnel cutting straight through the base of the mountain toward daylight. Clustered near the outer entrance are four blue markers.

"Guards at the perimeter," Illuna notes. "That confirms the passage is clear of cave-ins."

"How do we reach the floor with the main scaffolding destroyed?" Kate asks.

"During the drop, I spotted secondary rigging beneath the adjacent storeroom," Illuna says. "The timber looked intact."

"Can't you teleport us down?" Hadrik asks. "Like you did with Daren?"

"Once per day," Illuna says. "Same as the golden cage. One passenger, short range. It has no tactical utility for a full retreat."

Daren shifts his grip on his broadsword, looking away. "So you spent your only escape spell on me."

"It was the optimal call in the moment," Illuna says dismissively. "Do not flatter yourself."

Hadrik steps up to the dividing stone wall. "Stand back." He plants his feet and drives a heavy shoulder into the masonry. Mortar gives way with a grinding crunch, opening a jagged breach into the neighboring vault.

Beyond the rubble, the adjacent timber scaffolding clings to the sheer granite wall. Rungs and platforms descend into the gloom, weathered but solid.

"Move," Daren says, stepping through the gap. "Before the shaman cuts off the route."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Messenger, v_current_scene = Ch10-Messenger)


ID: Ch10-Scaffolding
TEXT:

 "How are we going to get all the way down there with the scaffolding broken?"

"During the drop, I spotted secondary rigging beneath the adjacent storeroom," Illuna says. "The timber looked intact."

"Can't you teleport us down?" Hadrik asks. "Like you did with Daren?"

"Once per day," Illuna says. "Same as the golden cage. One passenger, short range. It has no tactical utility for a full retreat."

Daren shifts his grip on his broadsword, looking away. "So you spent your only escape spell on me."

"It was the optimal call in the moment," Illuna says dismissively. "Do not flatter yourself."

"Are we sure the mines lead outside?" Kate asks. "A dead end traps us at the bottom of a pit."

"We copied the mine schematics from the archives," Illuna says. "Arraka, expand the projection."

"Hold your horses," Arraka grunts.

The glowing blueprint shifts. A lower subterranean network unrolls beneath the fortress layout, displaying a long, winding tunnel cutting straight through the base of the mountain toward daylight. Clustered near the outer entrance are four blue markers.

"Guards at the perimeter," Illuna notes. "That confirms the passage is clear of cave-ins."

Hadrik steps up to the dividing stone wall. "Stand back." He plants his feet and drives a heavy shoulder into the masonry. Mortar gives way with a grinding crunch, opening a jagged breach into the neighboring vault.

Beyond the rubble, the adjacent timber scaffolding clings to the sheer granite wall. Rungs and platforms descend into the gloom, weathered but solid.

"Move," Daren says, stepping through the gap. "Before the shaman cuts off the route."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Messenger, v_current_scene = Ch10-Messenger)


ID: Ch10-Messenger
TEXT:

 We scramble down the timber ladders in tight formation, boots thudding against damp rungs. The drop takes three tense minutes, but the structure holds. At the bottom, the cold air smells of sulfur and wet granite. Arraka's floating projection casts a crisp blue radiance over the jagged mine floor, charting every bend in the rock.

"One of the shaman's mages just vanished from the upper floors," Kate whispers, pointing at the blueprint. "A new blue dot appeared at the tunnel mouth."

"A runner," Illuna says. "Alerting the garrison."

"And they are cloaking," Arraka notes with a sneer. "Invisibility against me. How quaint."

"Can we fight them if we cannot see them?" Rose asks, clutching her staff.

"The cloaking spell shatters on physical contact or spellcasting," Illuna says. "It is an ambush mechanism, nothing more. Once steel clashes, they are visible."

Behind us, a cluster of red markers swarms the upper scaffolding, clambering down the ladders in pursuit. The green marker—the shaman—follows close behind.

"They found the breach," Hadrik grunts, loosening his dagger. "We have maybe two minutes before they drop onto our backs."

"Hold," Arraka's voice cuts in sharply from the locket. "Stop walking. Something is wrong."

"We cannot stop!" Daren snaps. "The entire stronghold is on those ladders!"

"I need five seconds, you thick-skulled ape!" Arraka snarls.

"Could it be a mechanical snare?" Rose asks.

"Unlikely," Hadrik says. "This is an emergency escape tunnel. Ogre garrisons do not booby-trap their own retreat line."

"And magical runes take time to etch," Daren adds. "A high-level ward should pulse against my aura."

"The vanguard is halfway down the shaft," Kate warns, her hand on her hilt. "We are out of time."

choice(""We could at least wait until the ogres reach the cave floor."", Ch10-Wait, v_current_scene = Ch10-Wait)
choice(""Screw Arraka! We can't afford to waste any more time here."", Ch10-Waste, v_current_scene = Ch10-Waste)


ID: Ch10-Waste
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_premonition < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_premonition >= 3)
#if(v_premonition < 3) {
 

"Screw Arraka! We can't afford to waste any more time here."

"Agreed," Daren says. "Run!"

We sprint down the dark corridor, boots splashing through shallow puddles. 

A terrible knot of dread twists in my gut. Arraka has no love for us, but she hates dying inside a crushed locket even more. If she called a halt, she sensed something real.

My boot lands on a flat slate tile.

A blinding flash turns the world white. The concussive blast hits like a physical fist, tearing the cavern roof apart. Five tons of granite crash down in a roar of thunder, burying us beneath the mountain.

}
#if(v_premonition >= 3) {
 

"Screw Arraka! We can't afford to waste any more time here."

"Agreed," Daren says. "Run!"

We break into a sprint. Ten paces in, a vivid premonition slams into my mind: my foot striking a hairline trigger in the floor, a blinding concussive detonation, and the ceiling collapsing into a tomb of crushed rock.

"Halt!" I roar, throwing myself sideways and skidding across the gravel. "Stop! Do not take another step!"

Everyone freezes, stone-still.

"I saw the blast," I pant, pointing at the innocent-looking flagstone ten feet ahead. "The whole ceiling caves in."

"There it is!" Arraka howls in triumph. "A concealed seismic ward! Pure concentrated force. Step on that and you get turned into paste!"

A faint rectangular aura of pale blue light shimmers over the ground ahead, painted by Arraka's projection.

Daren steps forward, his brow furrowed. "I sense nothing."

"Because the shaman disguised the magical signature beneath the earth's natural resonance," Arraka mocks. "Walk into it if you doubt me. I'd love to watch you pop!"

Daren raises his enchanted broadsword. The blade hums with a faint white luminescence as he traces three intersecting geometric arcs through the air, severing the invisible ley threads anchored to the floor.

A sharp crack echoes through the tunnel, like snapping dry kindling. A thin wisp of acrid ozone curls up from the stone, and the blue aura dissolves.

"Wards broken," Daren breathes, lowering his guard.

"Well done, metal man," Arraka purrs. "Now look up. Company has arrived."

"Clever," a deep, raspy voice echoes from the darkness ahead. 

The air wavers like heat haze. Six towering ogres materialize from empty air, their staves and iron weapons drawn. At their head stands the shaman, his ancient tusks bound in copper wire, his gaze burning with cold venom.

"You pierce my cloaking and dismantle my death runes. Now I see how you slipped past every guard in my fortress."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_premonition < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_premonition < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_premonition < 3)
choice("Continue", Ch10-Ritual, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ritual) if (v_premonition >= 3)


ID: Ch10-Wait
TEXT:

 "We could at least wait until the ogres reach the cave floor. They'll still need another five minutes to catch up to us after that, so we'll have plenty of time left to finish off the guards and exit the tunnels."

"Thirty seconds," Daren grinds out, eyes fixed on the scaffolding behind us. "Then we move."

"Got it!" Arraka snaps from the locket. "High-yield seismic trap, fifty paces ahead. Cloaked against aura detection. Step on that slate, and the entire cavern roof drops on your skulls."

A pale blue glow illuminates a rectangular patch of stone ahead, highlighted by Arraka's projection.

Daren advances carefully, broadsword raised. He traces three precise intersecting arcs through the air, his blade glowing with white purification magic, cleaving the delicate magical weave anchored to the stone.

A sharp crack snaps through the quiet, followed by a puff of acrid ozone smoke. The blue outline vanishes.

"Ward dispelled," Daren says, stepping across the safe stone.

"And here come the hosts," Arraka cackles.

"Clever," a raspy voice echoes from the gloom ahead.

The air shimmers. Six massive ogres materialize from the darkness, staves and blades ready. At their head stands the shaman, his tusks banded in copper, eyes fixed on us with icy malice.

"You pierce my cloaking and dismantle my death runes. Now I see how you slipped past every guard in my fortress."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Ritual, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ritual)


ID: Ch10-Ritual
TEXT:

 "Modest as ever," Arraka sneers, shifting the blue dot on her projection to a sharp green. "You masked your own aura with an anchor relic to slide past my senses. Quite the elaborate trick for an overgrown barbarian."

"An artifact worn by a decoy," the shaman rumbles, baring his teeth.

"Clever," Arraka says, swapping the original green marker back to blue.

"How did you track us here?" Illuna demands.

"The captain's soul was tethered to mine," the shaman says. "When his spark died, I knew where to strike. You will burn for your treachery."

He raises his gnarl-wood staff. Five ogre mages step past his flanks, casting clustered fireballs in unison.

Hadrik and Daren step into the breach. Hadrik drives his aura-reinforced fists into the leading two fireballs, deflecting them clean back into the ogres. Daren plants his boots, raising his enchanted blade vertically; the steel drinks the remaining three fireballs in a rush of white sparks, the crossguard glowing white-hot. With a heavy two-handed slash, Daren unleashes the absorbed fire in a roaring sheet of flame that engulfs the five mages, turning them to ash in seconds.

"Insolent vermin," the shaman growls, his eyes narrowing.

He slams the butt of his staff into the rock.

A wave of dense, freezing gray mist surges across the cavern floor. The stone beneath my boots dissolves. When the fog thins, the cave walls are gone.

We stand in a vast snow-covered clearing under a leaden, overcast sky. Silent flurries drift down through frozen pine branches. 

"I lost his aura," Arraka mutters, her voice unusually strained. "He cloaked again, but... I can't feel any of you either. My spiritual reach is completely severed."

"Mine as well," Kate says, testing the cold air. "The ambient mana is gone."

"A shamanic mind-realm," Illuna says, her breath misting. "A pocket dimension woven from the ethereal plane. Casting from the magical plane is barred here. Arraka and I cannot draw power, and physical ranged weaponry is suppressed—Barry's crossbow has vanished. Only self-contained relics like the shaman's staff retain their charges."

choice(""So, what's happening in the real world, right now?"", Ch10-Real, v_current_scene = Ch10-Real)
choice(""How do we get out of here?"", Ch10-Out, v_current_scene = Ch10-Out)


ID: Ch10-Real
TEXT:

 "So, what's happening in the real world, right now? Does the shaman plan to kill us while we're trapped in here?"

"No," Illuna says. "Time dilates within an ethereal pocket. A full year here elapses in less than a second outside. And the shaman is trapped in this realm alongside us."

"So it is a dream?" Hadrik asks, checking his knuckles. "We wake up if we take a fatal hit?"

"This is not a dream," Illuna says sharply. "Physical damage transfers directly to your earthen body. If you die here, your heart stops in the mines. Reconnecting to the material plane requires every soul in the realm to hold the exact memory of the cavern with absolute conviction. The shaman will never willingly align his intent. To escape, we have to kill him."

"Does he control this world?" Daren asks, scanning the pine line. "Like a lesser deity?"

"He built the boundaries, but he is bound by its laws," Illuna says. "He possesses no spells beyond whatever charges remain in his staff. If he is not attacking us directly, he is waiting for his construct to hunt us."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Wolves, v_current_scene = Ch10-Wolves)


ID: Ch10-Out
TEXT:

 "How do we get out of here?"

"Can we just pinch ourselves?" Hadrik asks. "Break the trance?"

"This is not a dream," Illuna says sharply. "Physical damage transfers directly to your earthen body. If you die here, your heart stops in the mines. Reconnecting to the material plane requires every soul in the realm to hold the exact memory of the cavern with absolute conviction. The shaman will never willingly align his intent. To escape, we have to kill him."

"And our real bodies?" Daren asks. "Are the guards slaughtering us while we stand frozen?"

"Time dilates within an ethereal construct," Illuna says. "A full year here amounts to a single second on the earthen plane. We have all the time we need. But we cannot leave until the shaman is dead."

"Does he command this realm?" Hadrik asks.

"He constructed the space, but he is bound by its physics," Illuna says. "No spells beyond the charges in his staff. If he retreated, he is letting something else do his killing."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Wolves, v_current_scene = Ch10-Wolves)


ID: Ch10-Wolves
TEXT:

 Low, vibrating growls ripple through the pines.

From the dark tree line, dozens of glowing red eyes cut through the falling snow. Massive wolves emerge onto the white clearing—coal-black coats, bristling hackles, their shoulders standing level with my chest.

Five of them burst into a full gallop across the snow.

Daren and Hadrik step forward to form a wall. Daren catches the leading beast on his kite shield, spinning to take its head off with a clean horizontal cleave. Hadrik steps inside a lunging bite, hammering his bare fist straight into the second wolf's jaw; teeth splinter with a sickening crack, and the animal collapses with a choked whine.

The third wolf veers toward Kate. She meets it without backing up, dipping beneath the jaws with fluid, predatory stillness. Her steel knife flashes once across the exposed throat. Black blood sprays across the snow as the beast crashes dead into a drift.

Her eyes have zero fear—only the cold, lethal focus of a hunter who survived alone in a jungle as a child by becoming more dangerous than the beasts hunting her.

Illuna somersaults over the fourth wolf's rush, landing lightly atop a snowbank. Daren closes the gap and drives his broadsword through its spine.

"The shaman ran," Hadrik says, pointing toward a single set of heavy boot prints cutting deep into the snow toward the western ridge. "He let loose his pack and pulled back so they wouldn't turn on him."

"If we move as a group, the pack surrounds us in the open," Daren says, blade dripping red. "One of us tracks the shaman and finishes him before he recharges his staff. The rest hold the line here and draw the wolves."

choice("Volunteer to go after the shaman", Ch10-Volunteer, v_current_scene = Ch10-Volunteer, v_ch10_kate_scratched = 0)
choice("Let someone else volunteer for the job", Ch10-Someone-else, v_current_scene = Ch10-Someone-else)


ID: Ch10-Volunteer
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_perception >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)
#if(v_perception >= 3) {
 

"I volunteer for the job," I say. "He is relying on staff charges. I can close the distance."

"Are you certain?" Daren asks, parrying another snapping maw.

"You need two heavy fighters on the line," I say. "Hold them here. I will end this fast."

I turn and sprint into the frozen pine woods, following the deep boot ruts in the snow.

The tracks run straight through the drifts, unhurried, without detours or signs of panic. The shaman made no effort to mask his path. 

Ahead, the tracks terminate abruptly in an undisturbed clearing between two ancient pines. No footprints leading out. No disturbed branches above. He is cloaked, waiting.

I scan the clearing from behind a frost-rimmed trunk. Four yards to the right of the dead-end tracks, a subtle depression mars the surface of the powder—not a fresh step, but an unnatural settling of snow around an invisible displacement.

A classic ambush: leave an obvious trail to bait a reckless charge, then strike from the flank.

I grip my dagger with both hands, drop low, and lunge straight for the displaced snowbank. The shaman panics at the direct charge; his concentration breaks, the cloaking spell dissolves, and he scrambles to level his staff.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 3 && v_perception < 3) {
 

"I volunteer for the job," I say. "He is relying on staff charges. I can close the distance."

"Are you certain?" Daren asks, parrying another snapping maw.

"You need two heavy fighters on the line," I say. "Hold them here. I will end this fast."

I turn and sprint into the frozen pine woods, following the deep boot ruts in the snow.

The tracks run straight through the drifts, unhurried, without detours or signs of panic. Ahead, the tracks terminate abruptly in an undisturbed clearing between two ancient pines.

I rush into the clearing, driving my blade forward—and strike empty air.

Behind me, the snow crunches softly.

I spin. The shaman stands six paces away, his staff blazing with condensed blue lightning, a vicious grin curling his tusks. A projectile of pure arcane force screams straight for my skull.

My reflexes kick in before my mind can process the flash. I drop flat onto the snow; the bolt scorches the air an inch above my ear, detonating against a pine trunk in a shower of splintered bark and ice. 

The shaman grunts in surprise, channeling another charge. I spring off the snow, closing the gap with my dagger raised.

}
#if(v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3) {
 

"I volunteer for the job," I say. "He is relying on staff charges. I can close the distance."

"Are you certain?" Daren asks, parrying another snapping maw.

"You need two heavy fighters on the line," I say. "Hold them here. I will end this fast."

I turn and sprint into the frozen pine woods, following the deep boot ruts in the snow.

The tracks run straight through the drifts, unhurried, without detours or signs of panic. Ahead, the tracks terminate abruptly in an undisturbed clearing between two ancient pines.

I charge the end of the trail, lunging forward with my blade—and strike nothing.

Behind me, snow crunches.

I turn, but the blue bolt from the shaman's staff is already burning through the air. It strikes me dead center between the eyes, snuffing out thought and life in a searing flash.

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Volunteer2, v_current_scene = Ch10-Volunteer2) if (v_perception >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_perception < 3 && v_reflexes < 3)


ID: Ch10-Volunteer2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch10_shaman,1) if (v_agility >= 2 || v_strength >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 2 || v_strength >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_agility < 2 && v_strength < 3)
achievement("Defeated ogre shaman",v_ac_ch10_shaman)
#if(v_agility >= 2) {
 

The shaman's staff sparks too slowly. I duck beneath the timber head and bury my steel hilt-deep into his chest.

The ogre coughs a spray of dark blood across my tunic. As I draw back to strike again, the snowy forest fractures like brittle glass. Space twists violently, dragging our minds back across the ethereal divide.

We stagger onto the damp gravel floor of the goblin mines, gasping as our souls slam back into our physical bodies.

A sickening pulse of emerald light flares around Illuna, sinking into her chest before vanishing. 

Ten paces away, the shaman collapses onto the gravel, coughing black blood as his skin cracks with dull gray fissures.

"The end, banshee," he rasps with a ragged cough. "For both of us. A soul-binding death curse. My life ends here, but you will not outlive me by an hour. The traitor falls with the fortress!"

His flesh crumbles into a heap of dry gray ash before our eyes.

"Damn it!" Hadrik roars, leaping forward, but his fist only scatters the ash across the stone.

Illuna's face goes pale, then hardens into a bleak, impassive mask. She slides down against the damp granite wall, folding her hands in her lap.

"Keep moving," she says, her voice eerily steady. "I will hold the rear. I have perhaps fifteen minutes before the curse stops my heart."

"No!" Rose cries, rushing to her side. "You cannot give up! There must be a counter-spell—"

Daren steps forward, his boots crunching on the stone. He draws his broadsword and raises the point toward Illuna's chest in silence.

Illuna looks up at the steel and lets out a dry, bitter laugh.

}
#if(v_strength >= 3 && v_agility < 2) {
 

A shimmering barrier of translucent force snaps into place around the shaman, locking my blade an inch from his robe. The ogre sneers down at me, channeling his staff for a point-blank blast.

A standard kinetic deflection ward. It stops momentum, not mass.

I plant my back foot, lock my shoulder, and drive all my weight into the strike. The blade shrieks against the field, vibrations rattling the bones in my forearm, before the barrier shatters like frosted glass. The dagger punches clean through the shaman's sternum.

He chokes on his own blood. Before he can fall, the snowy forest fractures like brittle glass. Space twists violently, dragging our minds back across the ethereal divide.

We stagger onto the damp gravel floor of the goblin mines, gasping as our souls slam back into our physical bodies.

A sickening pulse of emerald light flares around Illuna, sinking into her chest before vanishing. 

Ten paces away, the shaman collapses onto the gravel, coughing black blood as his skin cracks with dull gray fissures.

"The end, banshee," he rasps with a ragged cough. "For both of us. A soul-binding death curse. My life ends here, but you will not outlive me by an hour. The traitor falls with the fortress!"

His flesh crumbles into a heap of dry gray ash before our eyes.

"Damn it!" Hadrik roars, leaping forward, but his fist only scatters the ash across the stone.

Illuna's face goes pale, then hardens into a bleak, impassive mask. She slides down against the damp granite wall, folding her hands in her lap.

"Keep moving," she says, her voice eerily steady. "I will hold the rear. I have perhaps fifteen minutes before the curse stops my heart."

"No!" Rose cries, rushing to her side. "You cannot give up! There must be a counter-spell—"

Daren steps forward, his boots crunching on the stone. He draws his broadsword and raises the point toward Illuna's chest in silence.

Illuna looks up at the steel and lets out a dry, bitter laugh.

}
#if(v_agility < 2 && v_strength < 3) {
 

A shimmering barrier of translucent force snaps into place around the shaman, locking my blade an inch from his robe. I strike it with all my strength, but the kinetic recoil jars my wrist, sending the dagger spinning into the snow drifts.

Before I can recover, the staff discharges at point-blank range. A wave of searing white energy engulfs me, vaporizing flesh and bone in a fraction of a second.

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Curse, v_current_scene = Ch10-Curse) if (v_agility >= 2 || v_strength >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_agility < 2 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_agility < 2 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_agility < 2 && v_strength < 3)


ID: Ch10-Someone-else
TEXT:

 "I'm on it," Hadrik grunts, his boots digging into the powder. "I'll run the brute down before he can catch his breath."

"Go!" Daren shouts, swinging his shield to block a leaping hound. "We hold the clearing!"

Hadrik tears into the woods like an avalanche, vanishing into the falling snow.

The rest of the pack swarms from the pines, a dozen coal-black beasts rushing in unison. Daren cleaves through the vanguard in a spray of gore. Four wolves break around his flanks, zeroing in on Illuna. Rose stays tightly tucked behind Daren's armor, ducking under his sweeping guard without impeding his swings.

I draw my hunting dagger and step forward to meet the charge.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Catch, v_current_scene = Ch10-Catch)


ID: Ch10-Catch
TEXT:

#if(v_agility >= 3) {
 

I sidestep a leaping lunge, slipping inside the beast's guard to drag my blade clean across its throat. It crashes into the snow, thrashing in its own black blood. To my left, Kate's knife drives down into the skull of a second wolf with mechanical precision.

Behind us, Illuna is pinned against a sheer boulder, three wolves circling in for the kill.

"Illuna, catch!" Kate shouts, flipping her bloodied dagger through the air.

Illuna snatches the hilt out of the sky, drops into a flat slide beneath the leading wolf, and unzips its belly from throat to flank. She springs upward, kicks off the carcass, and drives the blade down into the second beast's spine. Landing in a fluid roll, she finishes the third with a clean thrust through the eye socket.

Three more wolves bypass Daren's broadsword, stalking toward Kate and me with bared fangs.

}
#if(v_agility < 3) {
 

I lunge with my blade, but the wolf twists mid-air, dodging the thrust and snapping its jaws inches from my throat. Before its teeth can sink into my shoulder, a steel dagger punches through its skull from behind. The beast drops limp.

Kate yanks her blade free, casting a cold, sharp glance my way.

"If you lack the training for predator combat, stay behind the line," she says flatly. "They will tear you apart."

She pivots and buries her knife into the neck of a second hound in one seamless motion.

Behind us, Illuna is pinned against a sheer boulder, three wolves circling in for the kill.

"Illuna, catch!" Kate shouts, flipping her dagger across the clearing.

Illuna catches the hilt out of the air, drops into a flat slide beneath the leading wolf, and unzips its belly from throat to flank. She springs upward, kicks off the carcass, and drives the blade down into the second beast's spine. Landing in a fluid roll, she finishes the third with a clean thrust through the eye socket.

Three more wolves bypass Daren's broadsword, stalking toward Kate and me with bared fangs.

}
choice("Give Kate my dagger", Ch10-Dagger, v_current_scene = Ch10-Dagger, v_ch10_kate_scratched = 1)
choice("Keep my dagger and fight the wolves myself", Ch10-Keep, v_current_scene = Ch10-Keep, v_ch10_kate_scratched = 0)
choice("Give Kate my dagger and help her by throwing rocks at the wolves", Ch10-Rocks, v_current_scene = Ch10-Rocks, v_ch10_kate_scratched = 0)


ID: Ch10-Rocks
TEXT:

#if(v_strength >= 3) {
 

"Take it," I tell Kate, slapping my dagger hilt into her open palm. "I'll break their formation."

"Understood," she says, advancing with twin blades.

She weaves between the first two wolves, cutting down both with lightning-quick thrusts to the throat and temple. As the third lunges for her exposed flank, I heave a jagged twenty-pound chunk of granite from the snow and hurl it with all my strength.

The boulder catches the wolf square in the skull with a wet, heavy crunch, crushing its head instantly.

Space twists like spun glass. The snowy plains fracture and dissolve, snapping our consciousness back into our earthen bodies with the force of a physical impact. We gasp, staggering on the damp gravel of the abandoned mines.

Hadrik stands over the broken staff of the shaman ten paces away.

}
#if(v_strength < 3) {
 

"Take it," I tell Kate, slapping my dagger hilt into her open palm. "I'll break their formation."

"Understood," she says, advancing with twin blades.

She weaves between the first two wolves, cutting down both with lightning-quick thrusts to the throat and temple. As the third lunges for her exposed flank, I heave a jagged rock from the snow and throw it at its head.

The rock clips the wolf's snout. It flinches, losing its momentum, and Kate spins to drive both daggers into its ribcage.

Space twists like spun glass. The snowy plains fracture and dissolve, snapping our consciousness back into our earthen bodies with the force of a physical impact. We gasp, staggering on the damp gravel of the abandoned mines.

Hadrik stands over the broken staff of the shaman ten paces away.

}
 A sickening pulse of emerald light flares around Illuna, sinking into her chest before vanishing. 

Ten paces away, the shaman collapses onto the gravel, coughing black blood as his skin cracks with dull gray fissures.

"The end, banshee," he rasps with a ragged cough. "For both of us. A soul-binding death curse. My life ends here, but you will not outlive me by an hour. The traitor falls with the fortress!"

His flesh crumbles into a heap of dry gray ash before our eyes.

"Damn it!" Hadrik roars, leaping forward, but his fist only scatters the ash across the stone.

Illuna's face goes pale, then hardens into a bleak, impassive mask. She slides down against the damp granite wall, folding her hands in her lap.

"Keep moving," she says, her voice eerily steady. "I will hold the rear. I have perhaps fifteen minutes before the curse stops my heart."

"No!" Rose cries, rushing to her side. "You cannot give up! There must be a counter-spell—"

Daren steps forward, his boots crunching on the stone. He draws his broadsword and raises the point toward Illuna's chest in silence.

Illuna looks up at the steel and lets out a dry, bitter laugh.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Curse, v_current_scene = Ch10-Curse)


ID: Ch10-Keep
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 4)
