ID: Ch10-Dagger
TEXT:

 "Here," I tell Kate, pressing my dagger into her hand. "You'll put it to better work."

"Understood," she says.

She advances with both blades, weaving through the snow with deadly grace. Her first thrust takes the leading wolf through the eye; the second shears through the skull of the second beast. The third lunges low, its claws tearing a deep bloody gouge across Kate's right thigh before she drives both blades into its throat.

She grunts, ripping a strip of linen from her sleeve and binding the wound tightly around her leg.

Space twists like spun glass. The snowy plains fracture and dissolve, snapping our consciousness back into our earthen bodies with the force of a physical impact. We gasp, staggering on the damp gravel of the abandoned mines.

Hadrik stands over the shattered staff of the shaman ten paces away.

A sickening pulse of emerald light flares around Illuna, sinking into her chest before vanishing. 

Ten paces away, the shaman collapses onto the gravel, coughing black blood as his skin cracks with dull gray fissures.

"The end, banshee," he rasps with a ragged cough. "For both of us. A soul-binding death curse. My life ends here, but you will not outlive me by an hour. The traitor falls with the fortress!"

His flesh crumbles into a heap of dry gray ash before our eyes.

"Damn it!" Hadrik roars, leaping forward, but his fist only scatters the ash across the stone.

Illuna's face goes pale, then hardens into a bleak, impassive mask. She slides down against the damp granite wall, folding her hands in her lap.

"Keep moving," she says, her voice eerily steady. "I will hold the rear. I have perhaps fifteen minutes before the curse stops my heart."

"No!" Rose cries, rushing to her side. "You cannot give up! There must be a counter-spell—"

Daren steps forward, his boots crunching on the stone. He draws his broadsword and raises the point toward Illuna's chest in silence.

Illuna looks up at the steel and lets out a dry, bitter laugh.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Curse, v_current_scene = Ch10-Curse)


ID: Ch10-Curse
TEXT:

 "My apologies," Illuna says with a ragged breath. "I forgot your sacred vow to purify the wicked. Go on then. Deliver your justice. If you strike cleanly, Flower might wake before the body goes cold."

"Quiet," Daren says, stepping into her guard.

"Daren, stop!" Rose screams.

Daren drives the point of his broadsword down into Illuna's right calf.

A blinding burst of pure white radiance erupts from the enchanted steel. A sickening hiss of dark green vapor boils out of the puncture, curling into the damp cave air before dissolving into nothingness.

Illuna gasps, staring down at her leg in disbelief. "You... what did you..."

"I shattered the curse," Daren says, yanking the blade free and wiping it clean on his mail. "I am an enchanter of the Eastern Continent. Dispelling a crude blood-death hex without drawing ambient mana is elementary craft. We are even. You pulled me out of the shaft; I pulled you out of the grave. We never speak of this again."

Illuna looks at him for a long, silent moment. "...Agreed." 

A pale blue shimmer coats her palm as she weaves water over the flesh wound, knitting skin and muscle whole in seconds.

"Illuna!" Rose throws her arms around the girl's small shoulders, sobbing in relief. "You are whole! No lingering hexes?"

"None," Illuna says, stiffening uncomfortably under the embrace. "Though we still have an entire fortress of ogres scrambling down the main shafts."

"Leave that to me," Hadrik grins. "Outside. Move, move, move!"

We scramble through the low tunnel mouth into the crisp night air at the base of the mountain. The instant we clear the threshold, Hadrik springs six feet into the air, cocking his right arm back like a siege engine.

He drives a thunderous bare fist straight into the natural stone arch above the entrance.

The mountainside shudders. Fractures tear through the cliff face, and with a deafening roar, twenty tons of solid granite shear away, collapsing into the opening and sealing the mine under a wall of jagged boulders.

"That's your master plan?" Daren asks, coughing through the rising dust cloud. "A rockslide?"

"Simple, elegant, effective," Hadrik beams.

"They have mages who can shatter stone," Daren counters.

"If they detonate those boulders from inside, the entire cavern network caves in on their heads," Illuna says, dusting off her tunic. "And only their captain possessed the physical mass to clear a collapse by hand. Sifting through that rubble with air currents will take hours. We have our window."

"Told you!" Hadrik laughs. "Come on, let's dig up our gear."

"Wait," Arraka's voice drifts from the locket. "Two cloaked scouts are sitting in the brush fifty yards down the eastern slope."

"Do they block our path?" Daren asks.

"No," Arraka says. "Just watching."

choice("Let them be", Ch10-Spare, v_current_scene = Ch10-Spare)
choice("Go kill them", Ch10-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch10-Kill)


ID: Ch10-Spare
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_kate_scratched == 0) {
 "Leave them," I say. "We have the chalice. Digging through brush to hunt scouts wastes time, and Arraka tracks them anyway."

"Aye," Hadrik says. "Let's grab our packs and put miles behind us."

We unearth our buried rucksacks from beneath the pine roots and set a hard, relentless pace away from the stronghold. For the first hour, we push through the dark timber without torches, keeping our boots quiet on the pine needles.

Once Arraka confirms no patrols are within three miles, we ease our pace. Above the canopy, a near-full moon casts silver light across the forest floor.

"Healer," Illuna's voice breaks the steady crunch of our steps. "Your vow bound you until we cleared the fort. We are miles beyond their perimeter. Do you intend to draw your blade?"

Daren stares at the trail ahead, adjusting the strap of his shield. "No. I... have no desire for duels tonight."

"Noted," Illuna murmurs, watching him with guarded eyes.

}
#if(v_ch10_kate_scratched == 1) {
 "Leave them," I say. "We have the chalice. Digging through brush to hunt scouts wastes time, and Arraka tracks them anyway."

"Aye," Hadrik says. "Let's grab our packs and put miles behind us."

We unearth our buried rucksacks from beneath the pine roots. While we strap our gear on, Illuna presses glowing palms against the deep claw gouge on Kate's thigh, knitting the torn flesh smooth with cool water magic.

Once ready, we set a hard, relentless pace away from the stronghold. For the first hour, we push through the dark timber without torches, keeping our boots quiet on the pine needles.

Once Arraka confirms no patrols are within three miles, we ease our pace. Above the canopy, a near-full moon casts silver light across the forest floor.

"Healer," Illuna's voice breaks the steady crunch of our steps. "Your vow bound you until we cleared the fort. We are miles beyond their perimeter. Do you intend to draw your blade?"

Daren stares at the trail ahead, adjusting the strap of his shield. "No. I... have no desire for duels tonight."

"Noted," Illuna murmurs, watching him with guarded eyes.

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Toast, v_current_scene = Ch10-Toast)


ID: Ch10-Kill
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_kate_scratched == 0) {
 "Cut them down," I say. "If they track our departure angle, they point the garrison in our direction."

"Sensible," Daren mutters. "Make it quick."

Guided by Arraka's coordinates, we flank the brush in silence. The two ogres realize they are exposed, dropping their cloaking with raised clubs, but Illuna's water whip snaps through the dark in a single horizontal arc, severing both heads before they can shout.

We retrieve our buried packs from the tree roots and begin the long march south toward the sacred woods. For the first hour, we push hard through the dark timber, putting miles of rough terrain between us and the mountain.

Once Arraka confirms the area is clear, we settle into a steady march under the starlit sky.

}
#if(v_ch10_kate_scratched == 1) {
 "Cut them down," I say. "If they track our departure angle, they point the garrison in our direction."

"Sensible," Daren mutters. "Make it quick."

Guided by Arraka's coordinates, we flank the brush in silence. The two ogres realize they are exposed, dropping their cloaking with raised clubs, but Illuna's water whip snaps through the dark in a single horizontal arc, severing both heads before they can shout.

We retrieve our buried packs from the tree roots. Illuna weaves water magic over the bloody lacerations on Kate's thigh, closing the torn muscle and skin without leaving a mark.

We set a hard, relentless pace away from the stronghold. For the first hour, we push through the dark timber, putting miles of rough terrain between us and the mountain.

Once Arraka confirms the area is clear, we settle into a steady march under the starlit sky.

}
 "Healer," Illuna's voice cuts through the quiet. "Your oath expired the moment we cleared the stronghold. Should I prepare for an attack?"

Daren keeps his eyes forward, adjusting his shield strap. "No. I... have no desire for duels tonight."

"Noted," Illuna says, her gaze lingering on his armor.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Toast, v_current_scene = Ch10-Toast)


ID: Ch10-Toast
TEXT:

 "It is a relief we encountered no tournament mages on the trail," Rose says, steering the conversation away from duels. "Constant skirmishes would have slowed us down terribly, especially with people attacking Kate on sight."

"Why target Kate specifically?" Illuna asks, glancing sideways. "Are you not all competitors?"

"My elemental signature leaks," Kate says. "Mages mistake me for an awakened banshee. Flower attempted to teach me aura suppression, but her explanations made no sense."

"The girl acts entirely on instinct," Illuna says dryly. "She cannot teach what she never studied. The principle is straightforward. You do not crush your spiritual output; you smooth the turbulent eddies until the surface mirrors ambient atmospheric mana. Like still water. Here, observe."

For the next two hours, as we march through the dark pines, Illuna coaches Kate through the fundamentals of spiritual dampening. Step by step, she demonstrates how to pull the jagged edges of a mage's aura inward, settling the outer resonance until it registers as nothing more than the faint, mundane presence of an ordinary human.

Hadrik and I listen in, testing the breathing patterns and focus exercises as we walk. 

By the time we halt in a sheltered glade beside a running brook, the change in Kate is striking. Her icy aura, once sharp enough to prickle the skin from ten paces, is completely quiet. Unless someone inspects her with dedicated sensory spells at point-blank range, she passes for a normal traveler.

We pitch camp in the clearing. Hadrik claims two of his spare tents have torn seams requiring repair, prompting Rose to invite Kate and Illuna into her tent for the night. 

When dividing the watch shifts, Kate, Rose, and Hadrik immediately and flawlessly vote in unison for Daren and me to take the opening rotation. The synchronized ballot is suspicious, but I decide to let it slide for now.

Hadrik roots through his pack, producing two earthenware jugs of dwarven ale, demanding a victory toast. The others decline, thoroughly exhausted from the infiltration.

choice("Offer to share a drink with Hadrik before taking the first shift", Ch10-Drink, v_current_scene = Ch10-Drink)
choice("Leave the toast for some other time", Ch10-Other-time, v_current_scene = Ch10-Other-time)


ID: Ch10-Drink
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch10_toast,1)
achievement("A toast!",v_ac_ch10_toast)
 Since no one else stepped forward, I join Hadrik on two flat boulders near the edge of the brook. He pops the corks on two heavy clay bottles with his thumbs, handing one across the gap.

"Giant custom," he says, tipping a splash of thick dark ale onto the moss. "A sip for fallen kin before the living drink. Dwarven barks throw me out of taverns when I do it on their floorboards, so it's a pleasure to drink under open sky."

He raises the bottle high.

"To the chalice," he grins. "And to breaking these blasted iron collars before the week is out."

We knock the earthenware necks together. I take a long swallow.

Liquid fire hits the back of my throat. It burns down my esophagus like distilled lamp oil, blooming in my chest with an immediate, dizzying heat. The strongest spirits in western port taverns feel like watered cider compared to this.

"First taste of dwarven mash?" Hadrik asks, chuckling as my eyes water.

"It has... character," I manage, coughing once to clear my lungs. "I wasn't expecting it to hit like a warhammer."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Politics, v_current_scene = Ch10-Politics)


ID: Ch10-Politics
TEXT:

 "That's the northern brew for you," Hadrik says, taking a massive gulp. "I spend half my years in the mountain taverns. Good folk, mostly. Most barkeeps know I'm a giant wearing dwarf skin, but they pour anyway. Losing my tab would crater their monthly margins, so they look the other way."

"Do giants and dwarves not get along?"

"Blood runs deep between our clans," he shrugs, watching the stream bubble over the rocks. "Old wars, ancient sieges. Peace holds on paper, but memories are long. Doesn't help when some hotheaded giant decides to smash up a border hamlet to prove a point. Our own clan heads hunt the rogues down, but you can't un-bury the dead. Scars stay scarred."

He takes another pull from the jug.

"Enough history," he grins, nudging my boot with his heel. "Let's talk about more interesting developments. What's the tale with you and Rose? You two have been trading quiet looks since yesterday."

choice(""Not at all. What makes you say that?"", Ch10-Makes, v_current_scene = Ch10-Makes)
choice(""I guess you could say that."", Ch10-Guess, v_current_scene = Ch10-Guess)
choice(""We have been getting closer, but not in the way you might think."", Ch10-Closer, v_current_scene = Ch10-Closer)


ID: Ch10-Makes
TEXT:

 "Not at all. What makes you say that?"

"Just caught the two of you whispering by the fire last night," Hadrik winks. "Thought there might be a spark. Not your type, then? Prefer someone with more frost on them, like Kate?"

"Leave Kate and Rose out of this," I say, leaning back on the stone. "The real mystery is you and Arraka. Have you set a wedding date yet?"

Hadrik roars with laughter, slapping his thigh.

"Now that's the question!" he bellows, taking another swig. "I've been looking for an opening, but the timing is tricky. Need to convince Illuna to loan me the locket for an evening so we can have a quiet dinner."

He pauses, and we both drink from the clay bottles.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Intervention, v_current_scene = Ch10-Intervention)


ID: Ch10-Guess
TEXT:

 "I guess you could say that."

"Ha! Knew it!" Hadrik grins broadly. "Caught you two by the embers last night, but I wasn't going to pry without confirmation."

"Keep it between us," I tell him. "Nothing is official."

"Discretion itself, lad," Hadrik rumbles, tapping the side of his nose. "I might look loud, but I keep clan secrets when it counts."

"Leaving Rose aside," I say, "the real mystery is you and Arraka. Have you proposed to her yet?"

Hadrik roars with laughter, slapping his thigh.

"Now that's the question!" he bellows, taking another swig. "I've been looking for an opening, but the timing is tricky. Need to convince Illuna to loan me the locket for an evening so we can have a quiet dinner."

He pauses, and we both drink from the clay bottles.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Intervention, v_current_scene = Ch10-Intervention)


ID: Ch10-Closer
TEXT:

 "We have been getting closer, but not in the way you might think."

"Ah, the complicated route," Hadrik says with a nod. "So you're holding out for someone fiercer? Kate, perhaps?"

"Leave Kate and Rose out of this," I say, leaning back on the stone. "The real mystery is you and Arraka. Have you proposed to her yet?"

Hadrik roars with laughter, slapping his thigh.

"Now that's the question!" he bellows, taking another swig. "I've been looking for an opening, but the timing is tricky. Need to convince Illuna to loan me the locket for an evening so we can have a quiet dinner."

He pauses, and we both drink from the clay bottles.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Intervention, v_current_scene = Ch10-Intervention)


ID: Ch10-Intervention
TEXT:

 "Jokes aside," Hadrik says, staring into the dark timber, "without Arraka's trick in the vault, that ogre captain would've cracked my ribs to powder. She'd gut me the second she breaks containment, no question, but I owe her a round. I've fought solo my entire life. Never had a warband, never watched someone else's flank. Being part of a crew... it's a decent change of pace. I didn't enter this tournament for the Magium anyway. I came for the fights and the stories. What about you, Barry? What do you do if you actually take the prize?"

"Use the Magium to become a mage."

"Aye, but afterwards? Once the power is in your hands, what comes next?"

choice(""I want to start going on adventures!"", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 1)
choice(""I want to gain fame and glory!"", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 2)
choice(""I want to use my magic to help people."", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 3)
choice(""I want to conquer the world!"", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 4)
choice(""I'm just in it for the money."", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 5)
choice(""I haven't really thought about that, yet."", Ch10-Magium, v_current_scene = Ch10-Magium, v_ch10_magium = 6)


ID: Ch10-Magium
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_magium == 1) {
 "I want to start going on adventures!"

Hadrik laughs, raising his jug.

"That's the spirit, lad! If you ever run low on uncharted ruins or want to see the frozen crags up north, find me. Assuming we survive the tournament, of course!"

"If you want adventures when this is over, you could always return to Varathia," Rose's voice drifts from the dark behind us. "I can write you a formal traveler's charter."

Hadrik turns with a grin. "Changed your mind about the toast, lass? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To the road, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
#if(v_ch10_magium == 2) {
 "I want to gain fame and glory!"

"Fame and glory, eh?" Hadrik grins. "A non-mage claiming the Magium will turn every tavern ballad on its head. You'll have every high-tier spellblade gunning for your title, but I'd wager you'll find an angle."

"Barry will manage just fine," Rose's voice drifts from the dark behind us. "He has reached this far against impossible odds."

Hadrik turns with a grin. "Changed your mind about the toast, lass? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To glory, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
#if(v_ch10_magium == 3) {
 "I want to use my magic to help people."

"Helping people, eh?" Hadrik chuckles into his beard. "Sounds suspiciously like Daren's speeches. Careful you don't martyr yourself into an early grave, lad. Dedicating every breath to strangers turns a man dull."

"That is uncalled for," Rose's voice breaks in from the shadow behind us. "Daren's vows carry weight."

"Never said I don't respect the man," Hadrik shrugs. "We just aren't built from the same timber. But you've come to join us, Rose? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To good deeds, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
#if(v_ch10_magium == 4) {
 "I want to conquer the world!"

"Is that right?" Hadrik chuckles, raising an eyebrow. "Then I'll hold off on proposing to Arraka. Sounds like you two were made for each other."

"Barry, you never told me you fancied Arraka!" Rose's voice calls out playfully from the dark behind us. "I can ask Illuna for the locket if you want a private confession."

"I prefer moving at my own pace," I tell her dryly. "I wouldn't want to overwhelm her."

Hadrik turns with a grin. "Changed your mind about the toast, Rose? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To world domination, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
#if(v_ch10_magium == 5) {
 "I'm just in it for the money."

"I find that hard to swallow," Hadrik grins. "Wandering across continents, dodging stillwaters and hydras, just for coin? There are safer ways to fill a purse, lad."

"He hasn't thought that far ahead," Rose's voice drifts from the shadow behind us. "He will decide once he has the power."

"Fair enough," Hadrik says. "Changed your mind about the toast, Rose? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To full pockets, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
#if(v_ch10_magium == 6) {
 "I haven't really thought about that, yet."

Hadrik chuckles, nodding into his beard. "Fair enough. No point plotting the roof before the foundation is laid. One bridge at a time."

"Exactly," Rose's voice agrees from the dark behind us. "Claim the magic first. The rest can follow."

Hadrik turns with a grin. "Changed your mind about the toast, lass? Plenty of ale left in the stash."

"No, thank you," Rose says, stepping into the starlight. "I only came to speak with Barry before turning in."

"Understood!" Hadrik heaves himself to his feet. "Take my seat. Time I checked my blankets anyway."

He raises his bottle one last time. "To the next step, Barry!" We clink the necks, drain the last drops, and he strolls toward his tent.

}
 "Barry, listen," Rose whispers, leaning in close as Hadrik disappears into his tent. She stops, peering at my face in the moonlight. "Barry, are you intoxicated?"

"Slightly dizzy," I admit. "Dwarven mash."

Rose stifles a soft laugh. "Wait here."

She slips over to her pack, returning a moment later with a handful of crushed crimson leaves. 

"Chew on these for thirty seconds," she instructs. "They clear the blood."

I take the bitter, peppery leaves and chew. Within half a minute, the hot fog in my head lifts completely, leaving my senses sharp and steady.

"Better?" Rose smiles, sitting on the boulder. "Now, as I was saying: you noticed the coordinated vote for the first watch shift."

"It wasn't subtle," I say. "What is going on?"

"Flower's stasis lasts until tomorrow night," Rose says quietly.

"Right. Illuna holds the body alone."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Unconscious, v_current_scene = Ch10-Unconscious)


ID: Ch10-Other-time
TEXT:

 We pack away the clay jugs and let the camp settle into quiet.

As I check my crossbow string by the stream, Rose approaches from the shadows, checking over her shoulder to ensure Daren remains out of earshot.

"Barry, listen closely," she whispers. "You noticed the coordinated vote for the first watch rotation."

"It was hardly subtle," I say, looking up. "What are you three up to?"

"Flower's stasis lasts until tomorrow night," Rose says softly.

"Right. Illuna holds the body alone."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Unconscious, v_current_scene = Ch10-Unconscious)


ID: Ch10-Unconscious
TEXT:

 "Illuna has not slept in over twenty-four hours," Rose says, her brow furrowing with concern. "She is exhausted, but she refuses to close her eyes. She believes Daren will strike while Flower cannot shield her. Travelling with us is dangerous, but alone she cannot maintain aura suppression in her current state."

"So you want me to keep Daren occupied while you and Kate get her to sleep?"

"Yes. Hadrik agreed to claim his tents are torn so Kate, Illuna, and I can share mine. Kate will keep watch over Arraka's locket so Illuna feels secure enough to rest."

I exhale slowly. "Daren is obstinate, but he is not blind. I doubt your scheme escaped his notice."

"Whether he guessed it or not, I need him away from our tent," Rose says firmly. "I know he would not murder someone in their sleep, but Illuna does not know that. I need you on watch with him. But if you are uncomfortable keeping secrets, I understand—"

"I am fine," I cut in. "I will keep his focus on the perimeter. But if he asks me outright what you are doing, I am not weaving elaborate lies. Deal?"

Rose smiles, offering her hand. "Deal."

"Get some rest," I tell her.

She slips back toward her tent. I check the latch on my crossbow, roll my shoulders, and walk over to the fallen log where Daren sits in the moonlight, methodically sharpening his broadsword with a whetstone.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Stubbornly, v_current_scene = Ch10-Stubbornly)


ID: Ch10-Stubbornly
TEXT:

 "Took you long enough," Daren grunts as I drop onto the dry grass beside his log. "Rose briefing you on how to keep me distracted while they tuck the banshee into bed?"

"Word for word," I say.

Daren pauses his whetstone, letting out a long, heavy breath. He stares down at the silver edge of his blade.

"Listen, Barry," he says, his voice unusually low. "I've been... difficult lately. More than usual. And for what it's worth, I am sorry."

Hearing Daren offer a genuine, unprompted apology feels about as natural as watching ice burn, but the exhaustion in his face is real.

"Don't worry about it," I say. "We made it out."

"It is not fine," he insists, shaking his head. "I clung to dogma. I convinced myself that banshees are incapable of anything other than betrayal, and I nearly forced a fatal duel over it. When clear proof stared me in the face, I doubled down until I was hanging over an abyss."

"You mean when Illuna reached down for your hand?"

"Yes," he murmurs.

"It wasn't just Illuna," I point out. "You've been coiled tight ever since Azarius explained artificial mages and the Beacon of Hope."

I pause, letting the night air settle between us.

"You haven't spoken a single word to Kate since that afternoon."

Daren freezes, blinking into the darkness as the realization hits him.

"You are right," he whispers. "I have completely shut her out. I don't know what came over me."

choice(""A lot of things are wrong with you, Daren."", Ch10-Wrong, v_current_scene = Ch10-Wrong)
choice(""Maybe you've been subconsciously resenting her for proving you wrong."", Ch10-Subconsciously, v_current_scene = Ch10-Subconsciously)
choice(""Maybe you'll feel better if you apologize to her."", Ch10-Apologize, v_current_scene = Ch10-Apologize)


ID: Ch10-Wrong
TEXT:

 "A lot of things are wrong with you, Daren," I say. "You are fortunate to have a forgiving friend like me to keep you grounded."

"You know," Daren mutters, "just because I am having a crisis of conscience does not mean I won't clip you with the flat of this blade."

"Point taken," I smile. "But you know what you need to do, don't you?"

Daren rubs the scar on his forehead. "Aye. I do."

He sheathes his broadsword with a soft click.

"Tomorrow, I clear the air. I doubt I can bring myself to trade pleasantries with the banshee, but I will show her the feud is done. And as for Kate... I will find the right words. Thank you, Barry. I needed that."

A few hours later, Kate and Hadrik emerge to relieve our watch. Kate gives me a quiet nod, confirming Illuna finally fell asleep, though she refused to dismiss her internal alarm and wakes every hour to check Arraka's locket.

At first light, we strike camp and push south toward the ruins. With the route memorized, we bypass Flower's trail markers and shave half a day off the return trek.

Surviving the fortress has forged an unspoken cohesion among us. Even Arraka participates from her unlatched locket, interjecting venomous commentary and Still Winter anecdotes between our debates.

Daren makes several stiff, formal attempts at small talk with Illuna, which only makes her regard him with deep suspicion. And despite his vows, he fails to utter a single word to Kate before the ancient stonework of the ruins rises above the trees.

The golden fox regards us with genuine surprise when Illuna presents the chalice. True to her word, Eleya dissolves the iron collars around our throats with a flick of her tail. Free of the lethal runes, we offer our farewells to the fox and the leopard, eager to leave the forbidden territory behind forever.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Elaria, v_current_scene = Ch10-Elaria) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)
choice("Continue", Ch10-Minimize, v_current_scene = Ch10-Minimize) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)


ID: Ch10-Subconsciously
TEXT:

 "Maybe you've been subconsciously resenting her for proving you wrong."

Daren frowns, troubled. "You think I hold a grudge against her for not being a monster? I know my pride gets the better of me, but... surely I would not..."

"The motive doesn't matter," I say. "What matters is what you do now."

Daren stares at his boots. "Aye. You are right."

He sheathes his broadsword with a soft click.

"Tomorrow, I clear the air. I doubt I can bring myself to trade pleasantries with the banshee, but I will show her the feud is done. And as for Kate... I will find the right words. Thank you, Barry. I needed that."

A few hours later, Kate and Hadrik emerge to relieve our watch. Kate gives me a quiet nod, confirming Illuna finally fell asleep, though she refused to dismiss her internal alarm and wakes every hour to check Arraka's locket.

At first light, we strike camp and push south toward the ruins. With the route memorized, we bypass Flower's trail markers and shave half a day off the return trek.

Surviving the fortress has forged an unspoken cohesion among us. Even Arraka participates from her unlatched locket, interjecting venomous commentary and Still Winter anecdotes between our debates.

Daren makes several stiff, formal attempts at small talk with Illuna, which only makes her regard him with deep suspicion. And despite his vows, he fails to utter a single word to Kate before the ancient stonework of the ruins rises above the trees.

The golden fox regards us with genuine surprise when Illuna presents the chalice. True to her word, Eleya dissolves the iron collars around our throats with a flick of her tail. Free of the lethal runes, we offer our farewells to the fox and the leopard, eager to leave the forbidden territory behind forever.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Elaria, v_current_scene = Ch10-Elaria) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)
choice("Continue", Ch10-Minimize, v_current_scene = Ch10-Minimize) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)


ID: Ch10-Apologize
TEXT:

 "Maybe you'll feel better if you apologize to her."

"Perhaps," Daren sighs. "Brooding by the fire certainly hasn't solved anything."

He sheathes his broadsword with a soft click.

"Tomorrow, I clear the air. I doubt I can bring myself to trade pleasantries with the banshee, but I will show her the feud is done. And as for Kate... I will find the right words. Thank you, Barry. I needed that."

A few hours later, Kate and Hadrik emerge to relieve our watch. Kate gives me a quiet nod, confirming Illuna finally fell asleep, though she refused to dismiss her internal alarm and wakes every hour to check Arraka's locket.

At first light, we strike camp and push south toward the ruins. With the route memorized, we bypass Flower's trail markers and shave half a day off the return trek.

Surviving the fortress has forged an unspoken cohesion among us. Even Arraka participates from her unlatched locket, interjecting venomous commentary and Still Winter anecdotes between our debates.

Daren makes several stiff, formal attempts at small talk with Illuna, which only makes her regard him with deep suspicion. And despite his vows, he fails to utter a single word to Kate before the ancient stonework of the ruins rises above the trees.

The golden fox regards us with genuine surprise when Illuna presents the chalice. True to her word, Eleya dissolves the iron collars around our throats with a flick of her tail. Free of the lethal runes, we offer our farewells to the fox and the leopard, eager to leave the forbidden territory behind forever.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Elaria, v_current_scene = Ch10-Elaria) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)
choice("Continue", Ch10-Minimize, v_current_scene = Ch10-Minimize) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)


ID: Ch10-Elaria
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch10_reunion,1) if (v_ch6_rose_deer == 1)
achievement("A touching reunion",v_ac_ch10_reunion)
#if(v_ch6_rose_deer == 0) {
 As we clear the outer perimeter, Leo the leopard calls out from atop a mossy parapet.

"Halt," his deep voice rumbles in Common. "Is one of you called Rose?"

"I am Rose," she says, turning back. "Why do you ask?"

"The doe Elaria has been pacing the southern ridge, waiting for your return," Leo says. "She was deeply troubled. Shall I deliver a message?"

Rose hesitates, her gaze softening. "Tell her... tell her I am safe. Tell her I am deeply grateful for her concern, and that I wish her peaceful seasons."

"It shall be done," Leo dips his head, padding back into the ruins.

}
#if(v_ch6_rose_deer == 1) {
 As we clear the outer perimeter, Leo the leopard calls out from atop a mossy parapet.

"Halt," his deep voice rumbles in Common. "Is one of you called Rose?"

"I am Rose," she says, turning back. "Why do you ask?"

"The doe Elaria has been pacing the southern ridge, waiting for your return," Leo says. "She was deeply troubled. Shall I deliver a message?"

"No," Rose says, stepping forward with quiet resolve. "I want to speak with her myself. Please ask her to meet me here."

"Very well," Leo purrs, leaping down into the brush.

"Facing old ghosts?" I ask quietly.

"About time, don't you think?" Rose smiles.

Ten minutes later, light hooves rustle through the ferns. Elaria trots into the clearing, stopping to catch her breath.

"Rose!" the doe calls in the ancient forest tongue. "I feared the worst! The animals carried word of your capture by the golden fox, and when I learned you were sent to the ogre fortress, I thought you were lost."

Rose steps forward, wrapping her arms around the doe's neck in a warm embrace.

"Thank you, Elaria," Rose whispers, tears glittering in her eyelashes. "Thank you for watching over me. I am so sorry for avoiding you after Molan's father passed. Guilt overwhelmed me, and I ran. I should have stood beside you. Can you forgive me?"

"Always, little one," Elaria nuzzles her cheek gently. "Our grove is your home. Never forget that."

"I will visit as soon as the town is safe," Rose promises. "And I will bring the sweet blue moss Molan loves."

"He will be overjoyed," Elaria says with a soft nicker. "Walk safely, Rose."

She turns and bounds into the green shade. Rose stands watching, her shoulders lighter than I have seen them in days.

}
choice("Continue", Ch10-Minimize, v_current_scene = Ch10-Minimize)


ID: Ch10-Minimize
TEXT:

 We establish camp on a grassy rise beside a wide river, miles beyond the forbidden border. Flower, newly awakened from her two-day stasis, listens wide-eyed as we recount the raid on the fortress.

"I'm so relieved Petal's plan succeeded!" Flower beams, sitting cross-legged in the grass. "I was nervous when she put me to sleep, but she told me to trust her."

"Petal's plan?" Kate asks, looking up from her boots. "I thought you formulated the strategy together."

"Well..." Flower fidgets with her hem, blushing. "Petal handles the tactics. I usually just tag along. At first she refused to get involved with you, but something changed her mind the night before the assault. She wouldn't explain, but suddenly she took over the whole operation and adjusted every route to keep you safe! I don't know what happened, but I'm thrilled it worked."

Rose catches my eye from across the fire, a faint, knowing smile playing on her lips. Our midnight conversation with Illuna had a far deeper impact than either of us expected.

choice("Tell Flower the reason why Illuna changed her mind", Ch10-Reason, v_current_scene = Ch10-Reason)
choice("Keep quiet in order to spare Illuna the embarrassment", Ch10-Embarrassment, v_current_scene = Ch10-Embarrassment)


ID: Ch10-Reason
TEXT:

 "I think I know what happened in that night that made her change her mind!" I say with a grin.

Across the fire, Flower's eyes flash vivid ice-blue as Illuna glares at me with pure venom.

"You do?" Flower asks, leaning in.

"Rose and I joined her on watch," I say. "Talked her ear off for two hours. She complained about her sandwich the entire time, but I think she secretly appreciated the company."

"That is so like her!" Flower giggles. "Petal never admits when she's fond of someone."

"Wait," Daren frowns at Flower. "You are saying you did not negotiate the battle plan with Illuna?"

"No, Petal handled the—" Flower stops, her jaw dropping. "Did... did you just call her by her real name?"

Daren shifts his weight, clearing his throat. "I suppose I did."

"Does that mean you're friends now?" Flower gasps, beaming.

"No!" Daren and Illuna bark in unison.

"Oh, it's true!" Flower claps her hands. "You're finally getting along!"

"Bickered like an old married couple the entire walk back," Arraka purrs from the locket. "Positively sickening."

Flower blinks at the gold pendant on her chest. "Wait... why is your latch open? Petal never leaves the locket unsealed!"

"A new era," Arraka sneers. "In recognition of my tactical brilliance, I have been elevated to senior council. Going forward, you will heed my advice. For instance, I suggest we decapitate five wild boars and mount their heads on pikes around the tents to establish territory."

"Do not push your luck," Illuna mutters.

"So much happened while I was asleep!" Flower scrambles for her canvas satchel, pulling out a charcoal stick and paper. "Hold on, I have to record everything!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Lemon-cakes, v_current_scene = Ch10-Lemon-cakes)


ID: Ch10-Embarrassment
TEXT:

 I keep my mouth shut and let Illuna retain her dignity.

"Wait," Daren frowns at Flower. "You are saying you did not negotiate the battle plan with Illuna?"

"No, Petal handled the—" Flower stops, her jaw dropping. "Did... did you just call her by her real name?"

Daren shifts his weight, clearing his throat. "I suppose I did."

"Does that mean you're friends now?" Flower gasps, beaming.

"No!" Daren and Illuna bark in unison.

"Oh, it's true!" Flower claps her hands. "You're finally getting along!"

"Bickered like an old married couple the entire walk back," Arraka purrs from the locket. "Positively sickening."

Flower blinks at the gold pendant on her chest. "Wait... why is your latch open? Petal never leaves the locket unsealed!"

"A new era," Arraka sneers. "In recognition of my tactical brilliance, I have been elevated to senior council. Going forward, you will heed my advice. For instance, I suggest we decapitate five wild boars and mount their heads on pikes around the tents to establish territory."

"Do not push your luck," Illuna mutters.

"So much happened while I was asleep!" Flower scrambles for her canvas satchel, pulling out a charcoal stick and paper. "Hold on, I have to record everything!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Lemon-cakes, v_current_scene = Ch10-Lemon-cakes)


ID: Ch10-Lemon-cakes
TEXT:

 Once the tents are staked and the fire burns down to embers, Flower offers to conjure dinner. Flush with reserve mana from her two-day nap, she takes custom orders. Hadrik demands spiced manticore ribs; Daren requests honey-glazed fowl. 

I settle for a plate of lemon cakes. Kate, whose palate never extended beyond field rations and raw trail kills, asks for simple roasted duck legs and retreats to a fallen birch thirty paces from the clearing.

I finish my cakes and walk over to her log. She sits staring through the dark pines, so lost in thought she only notices me when I step into her line of sight.

"Still turning over Diane?" I ask quietly.

Kate blinks, momentarily startled by the bluntness, before looking back down at the moss.

"Yes," she murmurs. "Ever since the ruins. I tried to keep my mind on the infiltration, but..." She trails off.

"Here," I say, offering the wooden plate. "Have a lemon cake. I had enough."

"Thanks," she takes one, turning it between her fingers.

choice("Tell her about how Diane defended us from the two lessathi, to help ease Kate's mind", Ch10-Ease, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ease)
choice("Ask her if she's still going to look for Diane after what happened", Ch10-Happened, v_current_scene = Ch10-Happened)


ID: Ch10-Ease
TEXT:

 "You should know," I say, leaning against a neighboring trunk, "after Diane's shock knocked you out, my lessathi resistance kept me conscious for another minute."

Kate looks up, her dark eyes sharp and focused.

"The lessathi wanted to slit our throats," I continue. "Diane stepped between us and their blades. She swore she would execute both of them if they touched you. She is the only reason we woke up alive."

Kate lets out a dry, bitter laugh.

"And also the reason we were collared and thrown into an ogre dungeon," she says. "I appreciate what you're trying to do, Barry, but I know Diane. She always convinced herself she was shielding me, right before her rash stunts dragged us both into a catastrophe. When I find her, I am going to break her nose."

"Will you stay in the tournament?"

Kate pauses, dusting sugar from her fingertips.

"I have no active tracks," she says slowly. "If tournament officials announce new trial mandates, I will have to participate to avoid elimination. Until then... I was hoping to travel with your group. That is... if you'll have me."

"Of course we'll have you, Kate," I say. "Daren has been distant, but—"

Heavy boots crunch on the pine needles behind us. Daren approaches the birch log, looking profoundly uncomfortable.

"Speak of the devil," I whisper.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Sulking, v_current_scene = Ch10-Sulking)


ID: Ch10-Happened
TEXT:

 "So, what do you plan to do now?" I ask her. "Are you still going to keep looking for Diane?"

Kate stares down at a trail of black ants winding across the birch bark.

"Yes," she says after a long silence. "I cannot abandon her just because she pushed me away. She always does this—shoulders every burden alone, convinced she is protecting me, exactly like she did when we were children in the facility. Her reckless choices nearly got us slaughtered. When I find her, I am going to break her nose."

"Will you stay in the tournament?"

Kate hesitates, tracing the grain of the wood with her thumbnail.

"I have no active tracks," she says slowly. "If tournament officials announce new trial mandates, I will have to participate to avoid elimination. Until then... I was hoping to travel with your group. That is... if you'll have me."

"Of course we'll have you, Kate," I say. "Daren has been distant, but—"

Heavy boots crunch on the pine needles behind us. Daren approaches the birch log, looking profoundly uncomfortable.

"Speak of the devil," I whisper.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Sulking, v_current_scene = Ch10-Sulking)


ID: Ch10-Sulking
TEXT:

 "Kate," Daren begins, stopping a few paces short of the log. "There is a matter I should have addressed days ago."

Kate tilts her head, deadpan. "You aren't proposing, are you?"

"No, confound it!" Daren snaps, flushing red before forcing his composure back into place. "I mean... my silence. I have not spoken a single word to you since the ruins."

Kate blinks, looking genuinely puzzled. "Have you? I hadn't noticed."

Daren visibly fights to keep his blood pressure down. He takes a slow breath, setting his jaw.

"After Azarius revealed you were an artificial mage from the Beacon, I froze," he says, his voice quiet. "I convinced myself from our first meeting that you were a banshee biding her time. When the truth proved me wrong, instead of owning my error, I retreated like a sulking child. I let pride govern my conduct. I know this comes late, but... I came to offer my apology."

He forces out the last word as if pulling his own teeth.

Kate leans forward slightly. "Pardon? You'll have to repeat that. The wind caught it."

"You heard me perfectly, woman!" Daren barks, throwing his hands up. "Do you accept it or not?"

Kate bursts into laughter—a clear, ringing sound without a trace of mockery or edge. Daren bristles for a second, then his shoulders drop and a rueful smirk tugs at his mouth.

"Of course I accept," Kate says, wiping her eyes. "I meant what I said—I never registered the silence. We were never gossips, Daren. But I appreciate the gesture. It means something."

"I have no idea what we're apologizing for," Hadrik's booming voice interrupts as he waddles over with five clay bottles tucked against his chest, "but any truce deserves a drink!"

He sets the ale on the birch log with a grin.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Tolerance, v_current_scene = Ch10-Tolerance)


ID: Ch10-Tolerance
TEXT:

 "Why not?" Kate smiles, taking a bottle.

"Hold on," Daren cautions. "We are without collars, but we remain in hostile territory. If roaming spellblades jump our camp while we are incapacitated, we have no defense."

"No need to fret," Rose says, stepping over with a small pouch. "These crimson leaves neutralize intoxication in thirty seconds flat. No dizziness, no morning sickness."

"Problem solved!" Hadrik beams. "Drink up!"

"Ooh, is that dwarven mash?" Flower calls out, eyeing the jugs. "I've always wanted to try dwarven ale!"

"Not for children," Hadrik says firmly. "Strong spirits stunt a growing frame."

"I am thirty years old!" Flower protests, stamping a bare foot.

"You possess the physical tolerance of an eight-year-old," Illuna's voice chimes in flatly. "Half a cup of cider leaves you weeping on the rug."

"Petal, you are not helping!" Flower huffs as the rest of us laugh.

The celebration stretches into the night under a canopy of stars. For a few hours, the terror of the tournament fades into the background—just friends sharing warmth, ale, and stories around a fire.

When I finally crawl into my blanket, I sleep undisturbed until ten the following morning. No night watch, no alarms.

I emerge from the tent to find the camp bathed in mid-morning sunlight.

"The sleeper awakes!" Daren calls out from the embers. "Enjoy your ten hours of luxury, Barry? Flower outdid herself with the breakfast feast, but fear not—I'm sure there are cold crusts left if you're desperate."

choice(""That's mighty generous of you."", Ch10-Generous, v_current_scene = Ch10-Generous)
choice(""I see it didn't take you long to turn into an ass again."", Ch10-Ass, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ass)
choice(""As tempting as that sounds, I think I'd rather go see if Flower is still taking orders."", Ch10-Tempting, v_current_scene = Ch10-Tempting)


ID: Ch10-Generous
TEXT:

 "That's mighty generous of you."

"A white mage's duty," Daren beams. "Service to my fellow man."

"The fact that you deliver that with a straight face is alarming," I say, pulling dried beef and an apple from my pack.

"Barry! You're awake!" Flower hurries over, looking crestfallen at my dry rations. "I told Daren to let me know the second you stepped out so I could conjure a fresh hot breakfast!"

"Daren conveniently omitted that detail," I say. "Don't worry about it, Flower. This is fine."

"Aww, but I researched a traditional dish from your home continent!" she pouts. "Quail eggs baked with cured ham and aged Bagelberry cheese!"

"That is less a traditional staple and more a luxury meal reserved for high nobility," I tell her. "Commoners in the west eat whatever survived the pantry mice."

"Oh," Flower blinks. "Well, good to know!"

Once everyone finishes eating, we assemble around the embers to map our route.

"We promised to escort Rose to her home in Thilias," Daren says, gesturing between Kate and me. "That remains our course. Hadrik, Flower—your debts are paid. You are under no obligation to travel south with us."

"Are you joking?" Hadrik laughs. "This crew is the most entertaining warband I've traveled with in fifty years. Official tournament trials haven't started yet. I'm sticking around."

"And you, Flower?" Kate asks gently.

"I..." Flower looks down at her bare toes. "I wouldn't want to intrude..."

"Intrude?" Hadrik snorts. "You fly, conjure gourmet feasts from thin air, and toss fireballs the size of wagons! Any band would kill to recruit you!"

"You are family now, Flower," Rose says softly. "We want you with us. What do you want?"

Flower's green eyes light up. "I want to come!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Chain, v_current_scene = Ch10-Chain)


ID: Ch10-Ass
TEXT:

 "I see it didn't take you long to turn into an ass again," I say. "Whatever happened to that profound soul-searching from last night?"

"That apology covered being unusually intolerable," Daren grins. "My current conduct sits squarely within standard operating procedure."

"Naturally," I mutter, pulling dried beef and an apple from my pack.

"Barry! You're awake!" Flower hurries over, looking crestfallen at my dry rations. "I told Daren to let me know the second you stepped out so I could conjure a fresh hot breakfast!"

"Daren conveniently omitted that detail," I say. "Don't worry about it, Flower. This is fine."

