choice("Continue", Ch10-Chain, v_current_scene = Ch10-Chain)


ID: Ch10-Tempting
TEXT:

 "As tempting as that sounds, I think I'd rather go see if Flower is still taking orders."

Flower spots me and beams, waving me over with a steaming porcelain plate.

"Ta-da!" she announces proudly. "A traditional Western specialty: quail eggs baked with cured ham and aged Bagelberry cheese!"

"Traditional?" I smile, taking the fork. "This is a luxury delicacy served only in noble high estates. Which makes it twice as impressive. Thank you, Flower."

"Enjoy!" she smiles, flushed with pride. "Let me know if you want seconds!"

Once everyone finishes eating, we assemble around the embers to map our route.

"We promised to escort Rose to her home in Thilias," Daren says, gesturing between Kate and me. "That remains our course. Hadrik, Flower—your debts are paid. You are under no obligation to travel south with us."

"Are you joking?" Hadrik laughs. "This crew is the most entertaining warband I've traveled with in fifty years. Official tournament trials haven't started yet. I'm sticking around."

"And you, Flower?" Kate asks gently.

"I..." Flower looks down at her bare toes. "I wouldn't want to intrude..."

"Intrude?" Hadrik snorts. "You fly, conjure gourmet feasts from thin air, and toss fireballs the size of wagons! Any band would kill to recruit you!"

"You are family now, Flower," Rose says softly. "We want you with us. What do you want?"

Flower's green eyes light up. "I want to come!"

choice("Continue", Ch10-Chain, v_current_scene = Ch10-Chain)


ID: Ch10-Chain
TEXT:

 "And Illuna?" Kate asks. "What is her verdict?"

Every eye turns toward Flower. The green in her irises shifts to clear, radiant ice-blue.

Illuna glances across the circle, crossing her arms defensively. "It... would not be an intolerable inconvenience to travel alongside you for a time."

"Settled," Daren claps his hands. "Pack up. We depart in thirty minutes."

"Excuse me!" Arraka's voice shrills from the locket. "Did anyone consult my schedule? Your group's kill-per-hour ratio is utterly pathetic! I demand a renegotiation of terms before I commit to another league of this dull wandering!"

We stare at the pendant in silence. Hadrik turns on his heel.

"Right then," he says. "Thirty minutes. Let's pull up the stakes."

"Hey!" Arraka screeches as we disperse. "Do not walk away from me! I am senior council! Come back here!"

Twenty minutes later, our rucksacks are lashed. As I return to the clearing, Flower stands twirling Arraka's gold locket on its chain like a pendulum, her brow furrowed.

"This brass link is wearing thin," she mutters. "We need a heavier steel chain in the next town."

"Fascinating metallurgy!" Arraka howls from within. "Now STOP SPINNING THE PENDANT!"

"Sorry," Flower tucks the locket under her collar. "I forgot about your motion sickness."

"Motion sickness?" Hadrik laughs, hauling his pack onto his broad shoulders. "The terror of the Still Winter gets dizzy on a chain?"

"The amulet distorts her sensory anchor," Illuna explains coldly. "Possessing physical hosts spared her that weakness."

"Kate is here," Daren says, checking his scabbard. "Southward to Thilias."

"What kind of town is Thilias?" Hadrik asks.

"A peaceful harbor city on the southern coast," Rose smiles. "Cobblestones, sea breezes, and fresh fruit. You'll love it."

choice("Continue", Ch10-Tracing, v_current_scene = Ch10-Tracing)


ID: Ch10-Tracing
TEXT:

 "Ready," Kate says, falling into line. "Let's move."

"Wait!" Flower gasps, clapping a hand to her mouth. "The dishes! I left the porcelain by the fire!"

"Leave them," Daren groans. "You conjure them from thin air."

"They sell for silver in the market!" Flower protests, already scurrying back toward the bank. "Two minutes! I'll rinse them in the river!"

"Tell me something, Daren," Hadrik says, watching her dash toward the water. "If you lot are white mages, shouldn't necromancers be called black mages? Keep the contrast clean?"

"A common layman error," Daren says, adjusting his pack straps. "The term has nothing to do with moral alignments. It derives purely from the visible white radiance produced during purification and restoration weaves. In fact, many high-circle masters consider the moniker vulgar, preferring—"

The air shivers.

A figure steps out of empty space directly between Daren and me. A slender youth, hands tucked casually into his trouser pockets, eyes closed, wearing a gentle, polite smile.

"Greetings, friends," Eiden says softly. "I come bearing news."

Daren freezes, his hand dropping instantly to his hilt. "Eiden..."

"Peace, healer," Eiden chides gently, tilting his head. "I came only to check on your progress."

"How did you track us?" I demand, my heart slamming against my ribs.

"Your spiritual resonance, of course," Eiden says. "I concluded my affairs on the eastern coast and decided to look in."

"Across the entire continent?"

"Auras are far more intricate than novice texts describe, Barry," Eiden says, his smile widening slightly. "Most practitioners read only brute power or basic fear. But every living aura carries an indelible imprint—race, true age, physiological decay, even deceit. With sufficient perception, one can trace a single soul across every league of Varathia. Or manipulate the spiritual weave to suspend physical aging entirely. Speaking of resonance... I observed your signature beside two lessathi several days ago. Did you deliver my message?"

Cold dread drops into my stomach. The two lessathi accompanying Diane. In the chaos of the ambush and capture, his errand completely slipped my mind.

choice(""I was unconscious at the time. I never even got to see the lessathi."", Ch10-Never, v_current_scene = Ch10-Never)
choice(""I'm sorry, I was barely even able to talk at that point. I'd just been electrocuted."", Ch10-Electrocuted, v_current_scene = Ch10-Electrocuted)


ID: Ch10-Never
TEXT:

 "I was unconscious at the time. I never even got to see the lessathi."

Eiden's smile remains pleasant, but his brow furrows slightly.

"Lying is unwise, Barry. Especially after I explained that deceit leaves an unmistakable ripple across your aura."

"Do you take us for fools?" Daren steps forward, his voice hard. "We know what happened in the Still Winter. Your message was a declaration of war. They would have slaughtered Barry on the spot."

"Naturally," Eiden says, unfazed. "The context was obvious. But an agreement is an agreement. Barry traded that delivery for your lives."

"We can find other lessathi," I say quickly, raising my hands. "We will deliver the words!"

"Barry..." Eiden sighs softly. "I am beginning to think you do not take me seriously."

"I take you very seriously!"

"This is unacceptable," Eiden murmurs, tapping his chin. "If you do not respect my terms, my authority means nothing."

"Listen to me—"

"I know," Eiden beams, opening his hands in an easy gesture. "I should execute one of your companions. That will correct your focus."

My throat tightens. "What did you say?"

Behind me, Rose lets out a strangled, desperate gasp.

"Rose!" Daren spins around.

Rose clutches at her throat, her knees buckling as she drops to the moss. Her mouth opens, but no sound emerges—only a horrific, dry whistle as her trachea collapses inward.

"Do not strain yourself, Rose," Eiden says mildly. "Your airways are sealing shut. Conserve what oxygen remains."

"Monster!" Daren roars, drawing his broadsword.

Before the steel clears the scabbard, Daren slams face-first into the dirt, paralyzed solid. Beside him, Hadrik and Kate drop like felled timber, locked in stone immobility.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Ending, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ending)


ID: Ch10-Electrocuted
TEXT:

 "I'm sorry, I was barely even able to talk at that point. I'd just been electrocuted."

"True," Eiden nods thoughtfully. "I felt your nervous system faltering from across the province."

"Exactly!" I say, pressing the opening. "I was barely coherent—"

"And yet," Eiden interrupts smoothly, "had my commission remained your absolute priority, you would have found the resolve to speak."

"We can find other lessathi," I say quickly, raising my hands. "We will deliver the words!"

"Barry..." Eiden sighs softly. "I am beginning to think you do not take me seriously."

"I take you very seriously!"

"This is unacceptable," Eiden murmurs, tapping his chin. "If you do not respect my terms, my authority means nothing."

"Listen to me—"

"I know," Eiden beams, opening his hands in an easy gesture. "I should execute one of your companions. That will correct your focus."

My throat tightens. "What did you say?"

Behind me, Rose lets out a strangled, desperate gasp.

"Rose!" Daren spins around.

Rose clutches at her throat, her knees buckling as she drops to the moss. Her mouth opens, but no sound emerges—only a horrific, dry whistle as her trachea collapses inward.

"Do not strain yourself, Rose," Eiden says mildly. "Your airways are sealing shut. Conserve what oxygen remains."

"Monster!" Daren roars, drawing his broadsword.

Before the steel clears the scabbard, Daren slams face-first into the dirt, paralyzed solid. Beside him, Hadrik and Kate drop like felled timber, locked in stone immobility.

choice("Continue", Ch10-Ending, v_current_scene = Ch10-Ending)


ID: Ch10-Ending
TEXT:

 "No one can intervene this time, Barry," Eiden says, his tone conversational. "What will you do?"

Cold panic suffocates my chest. The collars are gone, we survived the ogres, we made it out—and now this monster is crushing Rose's throat without lifting a finger. I have no stat boosts left. No loopholes. No leverage. 

"Her pulse is slowing," Eiden notes gently. "Convince me."

My mind races in blind circles. Words, bargains, ancient lore—nothing stops a being who commands the island's ley lines like breathing.

Crash.

Porcelain shatters against river stone.

Rose draws in a violent, rattling gasp of air as the invisible vice on her throat snaps open.

On the riverbank, Flower stands frozen among the shards of her dropped plates, staring at Eiden in unvarnished horror.

"Well," Eiden smiles toward the water, his closed eyes crinkling. "If it isn't Illuna of the sacred woods massacre. Twenty years, is it not? How have you fared?"

Flower's small frame quivers. The childlike confusion in her face twists into absolute, incandescent fury. Her green eyes ignite into searing, blood-red coals; waves of raw, scalding heat radiate off her skin.

"You!" she screams, her voice cracking with grief. "I'll kill you! I'll kill you!"

She launches herself across the clearing, hurling a tempest of white-hot fireballs. Eiden drifts backward on the breeze, slipping between the blasts without raising his hands. A concentrated beam of crimson flame erupts from Flower's palms, swallowing him whole—yet the fire parts around his silhouette like water against a prow, leaving his clothes uncharred.

"You butchered them!" Flower howls, tears evaporating on her burning cheeks. "Every single one! Bring them back! Bring them back!"

She conjures a roaring wyvern of solid fire that swoops from the canopy, raking fiery talons across Eiden's guard while Flower closes in with flaming fists.

Eiden parries the wyvern's claws with his left palm, swatting Flower's strikes with his right as if brushing aside leaves.

"Illuna," Eiden murmurs, his smile fading into mild annoyance. "Rein in your host. My patience has limits."

Flower's eyes flicker from blood-red to brilliant ice-blue. Pure terror flashes across her features.

Instantly, Flower's body locks rigid, frozen mid-strike by an internal command.

"Let me go, Petal!" Flower screams through gritted teeth, struggling against her own limbs as tears stream down her face. "Let me go! He has to die!"

The resistance breaks. Flower collapses to her knees in the grass, sobbing, turning her tear-streaked face toward us.

"It's him," she chokes out, pointing a trembling hand at the smiling youth. "He destroyed the city of Olmnar."

choice("Next chapter", Ch11a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch11a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)
